(function (root) {
	'use strict';
	var HE = root.HE = root.HE || {};
	HE.GUIDE = {"front":[{"id":"about","title":"About the course","html":"<p>Home, bonds, molecules and compounds</p><p>A practical bass course in note choice and harmonic recognition</p><p>Jack Elliot · The Leith Bass Space · Revised teaching draft · September 2026</p><p>This course develops two connected skills: hearing familiar chord relationships and choosing notes that make those relationships audible on the bass. Work through it alongside repertoire. Use a short progression to isolate a skill, then take that skill straight back into a tune.</p><p>The ten stages form a long-term course and reference book, not ten lessons. Start with a small set of elements that fits the music you want to play. A convincing bassline, a recognisable sound and a clear sense of the form matter more than completing every permutation.</p><p>You should be able to keep a simple pulse, locate notes on the bass and build major and minor triads slowly. If these are still developing, use Stage 1 with your teacher and work from TAB while connecting it to the stave. No fluent sight-reading or singing is assumed.</p>"},{"id":"organised","title":"How this document is organised","html":"<p>The overview table is a reference map in teaching order. You do not need to learn it before playing. Begin with the method and a selected core element, then return to the table to compare related sounds.</p><p>Part One, The Method, explains the working vocabulary, note choices and practice system. Read the core route and choose one achievable task before opening the full menu.</p><p>Part Two, The Elements, works through ten stages. Each stage opens with what it is about, then gives every element a card: the chords in C, what they fix and leave open, how the roots move, what to listen for, what to contrast it with, and where to hear it. Each stage closes with its featured drill, a checkpoint and the backing tracks it needs.</p><p>Part Three, Reference, gathers the lineages (how elements descend from one another), a short reading of the material by genre, the backing track plan, and the appendices of keys, scales and worked examples.</p>"},{"id":"conventions","title":"Conventions","html":"<p>Most examples use C as the tonal centre, major or minor, so you can compare them directly. Exceptions are identified explicitly. Roman numerals describe chord roots relative to the major scale on that tonic: in C minor, E♭ is ♭III and A♭ is ♭VI. So ♭III is always a minor third above home, whether the key is major or minor: in C minor it belongs to the key, and in C major it is a colour. A key is home and whether home is major or minor. Upper case means major; lower case means minor; 7, maj7 and ø7 specify seventh-chord quality. Dø7 means Dm7♭5. A dominant-seventh chord type does not automatically have dominant function.</p><p>Degree numbers in the element cards are relative to the tonal centre: in C, E is 3 and E♭ is ♭3. In arpeggio drills and Appendix C, degrees are relative to the current chord root: the third of G7 is B, which is degree 7 of C. Say which reference you are using.</p><p>“Fixed” means present somewhere in the listed chords; “open” means that degree is not specified by those chord symbols. The tables show a combined inventory, not a scale to use indiscriminately over every chord. If both ♭7 and 7 appear, locate each in time. The base voicing and expanded voicing rows name the chords actually counted; some base voicings already contain sevenths.</p><p>An open degree is not an unrestricted choice. Melody, extensions, voicing, bass register and style may settle it. “None” in the open column means every degree is represented, not that chromatic passing notes or alternative colours are forbidden. Cmaj7–Fmaj7 supports B♮; testing B♭ belongs first over the triads C–F.</p><p>Listening leads are starting points for comparison, not certified transcriptions. Some identify a short passage or an arrangement variant rather than a whole-song progression. Before assigning one, record the artist/version, section, timestamp, actual key, bass notes and chord durations. Use the supplied teaching loop when a recording has not yet been checked. Do not infer a recording’s key from the C-based examples.</p>"}],"method":[{"id":"method-the-idea","title":"1. The idea","html":"<p>The course uses a small working vocabulary for recurring harmonic structures. These are teaching metaphors, not a scientific classification or a replacement for established terms such as tonic, dominant and voice leading.</p><p>Home is the tonal centre: the note and chord that feel like a point of reference or arrival. A bond is a relationship between two chords. Looping a bond lets you hear that relationship repeatedly, whether it feels settled, directional or ambiguous. A molecule is a short progression, usually three to five chords. A compound joins these into a larger form, such as a twelve-bar blues. A transformation changes a familiar structure by altering, adding, replacing or reordering notes or chords.</p><p>Fluent players often recognise a familiar progression before they could explain every note in it. This course trains that recognition deliberately: listen, sing, play, compare and use the same small structures in different keys and musical settings. Analysis helps you check what you hear; repetition connects that understanding to a reliable physical response.</p><p>The two goals, held together. In the ear means hearing the element coming and knowing where it is going. Under the hands means the fingers already know the shape when it arrives. Ear without hands is frustrating, hands without ears is pattern playing, and the gap between the two is where most players get stuck.</p><p>The course is ordered by relationship because the same progressions recur across blues, soul, funk, jazz, rock and pop. Each stage introduces a small addition to material already encountered. Rhythm and arrangement can transform the effect of a progression, while harmony, melody, timbre and tradition also contribute to style. Rotate musical feels throughout the course rather than saving them for separate genre units.</p><p>Inspired by Janek Gwizdala's ii V I book, which drills the degrees of each chord through the changes. This extends that method across a wider set of progressions, a fuller menu of note choices, and a structure built on relationships.</p>"},{"id":"method-the-relationships","title":"2. The relationships","html":"<p>Every chord in an element is named by what it does in the key. Home is the chord of rest. Lean and pull are the two sides of home: lean places a chord on the IV side, pull on the V side. A shadow is a chord of the key that stands in for one of those three; it sits a third from the chord it replaces and shares two notes with it. A colour is a chord from outside the key that brings one note of another mode. An applied chord is a pull aimed at a chord other than home. This is still a partial map: same-root changes, tritone relationships and inversions need describing on their own terms. The common-tone counts below apply to the listed triads, not to every chord with those root distances.</p><div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Relationship</th><th scope=\"col\">In a major key</th><th scope=\"col\">In a minor key</th><th scope=\"col\">Shared notes</th><th scope=\"col\">What the ear reports</th><th scope=\"col\">Fixes, and leaves open</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Lean</td><td>IV</td><td>iv</td><td>One, with home</td><td>Away and back; often plagal.</td><td>Major I–IV adds 4 and 6; 2 and 7 remain open.</td></tr><tr><td>Pull</td><td>V</td><td>v, or V with the raised seventh</td><td>One, with home</td><td>Often directional; quality and context matter.</td><td>Major I–V adds 2 and 7; 4 and 6 remain open.</td></tr><tr><td>Shadow</td><td>vi and iii for home, ii for lean, iii for pull</td><td>♭III for home, ♭VI for lean, ♭VII for pull</td><td>Two, with the chord it stands in for</td><td>Often smooth, with little voice movement.</td><td>Listed diatonic triads usually leave three degrees open against home.</td></tr><tr><td>Colour</td><td>♭VII and v (♭7, Mixolydian); iv, ♭VI and ♭III (from minor); II (♯4, Lydian)</td><td>IV (♮6, Dorian); ♭II (♭2, Phrygian)</td><td>Varies</td><td>One note from outside the key: a new colour around the same home.</td><td>Fixes the note that names the mode.</td></tr><tr><td>Applied</td><td>II7, VI7, III7, and I7 into IV; ♭II7 in place of V</td><td>The same, aimed at the chords of the minor key</td><td>Varies</td><td>A pull towards something that isn't home.</td><td>Adds one chromatic note: the leading tone of the chord it aims at.</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>The counts help explain why some voicings connect smoothly, but they do not measure emotional intensity or determine function. A C–E♭ chromatic mediant shares one triad tone, not two; Cm–D♭ shares none, while Cm7–D♭maj7 shares C. Count the notes actually sounding.</p><p>In the major-triad examples, I–IV supplies 4 and 6 while I–V supplies 2 and 7. Together C, F and G contain all seven notes of C major. This makes I–IV–V a useful complete collection to study. It does not prove C is home: duration, melody, phrase endings and context establish the tonal centre.</p><p>Course names describe what a chord does in the key; they are not always formal harmonic functions. For example, ii is a shadow of IV by its notes and a predominant by function, although its root is a step from home. Chromatic mediants (I ♭III, I III, I ♭VI) are counted with the colours, although they don't all come from one mode.</p><p><b>Keys and numerals.</b> A key is home and whether home is major or minor. A major key's notes are the major scale's; a minor key's are natural minor's, with the raised seventh as well. Numerals always count from home on the major scale, so a numeral means the same distance from home in every key: ♭III is three frets up from home. Many loops can be heard from more than one home, and the Dojo accepts every hearing the course allows.</p>"},{"id":"method-the-three-questions","title":"3. The three questions","html":"<p>Every element in the course is approached with the same three questions. Together they join ear, theory and improvisation into one act.</p><ol><li>Which degrees are fixed? The notes the chords contain.</li><li>Which degrees are open? The notes the chords leave unstated.</li><li>Which choice fits the open degrees here? First check the melody and chord voicings, then test a familiar collection and listen to its effect.</li></ol><p>Over the triads C–F, the chords specify C, E, F, G and A, leaving D and B unspecified. With D natural, choosing B gives C Ionian; choosing B♭ gives C Mixolydian. Over Cm–F, A natural distinguishes the sound from Cm–Fm. Add D and B♭ and you have C Dorian. One characteristic note is a useful clue, but the complete collection and tonal centre determine the mode.</p><p>Adding sevenths or extensions specifies more pitches. When a progression contains a complete diatonic collection, begin by targeting chord tones on each change rather than searching for a new parent scale. Colour remains a choice: a note can belong to the key yet create tension over the current chord, and chromatic notes can still work as deliberate approaches or passing tones.</p>"},{"id":"method-two-more-lenses","title":"4. Two more lenses","html":"<p><b>Shared and moving tones.</b> When two chords meet, some notes stay and some move. The moving tone is what the ear picks up on, and it is the most natural target for a melody. Singing the moving tone before the change arrives is one of the core ear tests.</p><p>Whose home is it? Cm7–F7 can support C Dorian, or function as ii–V in B♭ with the tonic withheld. Dm–Am can be i–v in D minor or iv–i in A minor. Chord membership alone cannot settle the analysis. Listen to phrase endings, duration, melody and bass emphasis; a later cadence can clarify the hearing, but a modal centre does not require a cadence. Sometimes more than one hearing remains plausible.</p>"},{"id":"method-reading-a-molecule-ingredients-and-motion","title":"5. Reading a molecule: ingredients and motion","html":"<p>A molecule is described in two ways, and they answer different questions.</p><p>Ingredients name what each chord does in the key (home, lean, pull, shadow, colour, applied). They tell you which bonds you already own, and so which fixed and open degrees you already know. I vi IV V is home, shadow, lean and pull.</p><p>Motion describes the interval between neighbouring roots. Falling fifths often support cadential momentum; rising fifths can support a plagal return such as IV–I. Third-related diatonic triads often share two notes, and the step-related triads used here often share none. These are tendencies in the examples, not universal laws. Root motion and actual bass motion differ when a chord is inverted. A falling fifth and rising fourth reach the same pitch class, although their sounding contour differs.</p><p>The two descriptions have to be kept separate for one reason. Lean and pull are about home, not about root motion. I to IV and V to I both fall a fifth, which is exactly why the whose home question exists.</p>"},{"id":"method-how-each-unit-runs","title":"6. How each unit runs","html":"<p>Every element is worked through the same six steps. Asking the same questions in the same order is what makes the relationships between elements visible.</p><ol><li><b>Hear.</b> Backing track only, no instrument. Find home and sing it. Say what the element feels like before naming anything.</li><li><b>Map.</b> Fixed and open degrees, shared and moving tones, ingredients and motion. Say whose home it could be.</li><li><b>Play.</b> Climb the note menu, starting with the stage's featured drill.</li><li><b>Contrast.</b> Drill the element back to back against a neighbour already owned, usually one that differs by a single note.</li><li><b>Test.</b> Randomised recognition: name the element from a quiz track, sing the moving tone, name the choice made for an open degree.</li><li><b>Use.</b> Make music from it in several feels, and find it in repertoire. If an element never becomes a bassline or a solo it has not landed.</li></ol>"},{"id":"method-the-practice-regimen","title":"7. The practice regimen","html":"<p>Use readiness rather than a fixed timetable. This is a long-term syllabus: elements differ greatly in difficulty, and a stage may take several lessons or months. Begin with one useful bond and one key. Add a contrasting version, use it in repertoire, then expand the key range. The full course can remain a reference while a shorter teaching block follows a selected route.</p><p>Keys in the cycle of fourths: C, F, B♭, E♭, A♭, D♭, G♭, B, E, A, D, G. Each new tonic sits a fourth above the last, which is the same relationship as V to I, so the practice order is itself a series of resolutions.</p><p>Do not attempt every element, menu level and key at once. First learn the core task in C and one contrasting key. Extend to three keys, then take the featured drill around all twelve over successive sessions. Explore the fuller note menu only where it serves a musical goal. Twelve-key fluency is a consolidation target, not the entrance test for every new idea.</p><p>Sing before playing at least once per task, in a comfortable octave. If you cannot find the note, play it, sing it back, wait briefly and try again without the instrument. The aim is to build a sound-to-action connection, not to turn singing into another pass-or-fail barrier.</p><p>Split across the week. Where there are two sessions, the first hears, maps and drills. The second contrasts, tests and applies.</p><p>Revisit one older element at the start of each session. Mix it with the new contrast once each is recognisable in isolation, and return to it in a later lesson. Keep a note of errors so review time goes to the distinctions that still need work.</p><p>Name elements out loud in repertoire work, then play the passage without talking. Record the version and bar or timestamp in your listening log. The test is whether the recognition helps you enter, support the phrase and recover when the form changes.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">A workable 25-minute session</h3><p>Spend 3 minutes hearing and singing an older element, 5 minutes mapping the new contrast, 7 minutes on one playable drill, 5 minutes making a bassline and 5 minutes recording and reviewing a short take. Reduce the task before increasing speed. Stop a fingering or stretch that creates pain; choose another position.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Choose a route</h3><p>Core route: home and triads; I–IV against i–IV and i–iv; I–V against i–v and i–V; ii–V–I; one blues form; I–vi and the pop axis; i–♭VII; then one transformation used in a current tune. Treat the other elements as extensions. Revisit the core with sevenths once the triads are secure.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Use three levels of readiness</h3><p>Recognise: identify the taught contrast and sing or match its target note. Play: keep the pulse through four loops using roots and one planned target. Apply: make a short bassline and find a related passage in repertoire. A useful local checkpoint is four correct identifications from five fresh examples on two different days; it is a teaching target, not a validated ability score. Practise first in two keys. Broader key coverage and unannounced tests belong to later consolidation.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Practice record</h3><p>After each session record: element and voicing; key; tempo and click placement; task; one thing heard; one thing to revisit; repertoire link or timestamp. Keep a 30–60-second recording when useful. The next session should have a specific starting point.</p>"},{"id":"method-the-note-menu","title":"8. The note menu","html":"<p>The element is the frame. The menu is what goes inside it.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">The vertical ladder (chord by chord)</h3><ul><li><b>Level 0.</b> Roots in long notes. Keep counting while you hear the changes. Match note length to the harmonic rhythm: a whole note fits only when the chord lasts a full bar of 4/4.</li><li><b>Level 1.</b> Roots, in time. Add a pulse and a feel. This is already a bassline.</li><li><b>Level 2.</b> Root, fifth and octave: 1–5–8. This line does not state the third, so it leaves chord quality to the accompaniment. Use it to hear root motion and practise clean muting.</li><li><b>Level 3.</b> 1 3 5. The triad. The moment the third arrives, major or minor is declared. Name that out loud.</li><li><b>Level 4.</b> Root, third, fifth and seventh. Spell the actual chord: Cmaj7 is 1–3–5–7; C7 is 1–3–5–♭7; Cm7 is 1–♭3–5–♭7; Cø7 is 1–♭3–♭5–♭7. Later lists use 3, 5 and 7 as chord-member labels, with the quality understood.</li><li><b>Level 5.</b> Rearrange the chord tones. Begin arpeggios on the third, fifth or seventh, ascending and descending, then try a few scrambles such as 5–3–7–1. Starting an exercise on the third does not invert the band’s harmony: an inversion is determined by the lowest sounding chord tone.</li><li><b>Level 6.</b> The tenth: the third an octave higher. Try 1–10, then 1–5–10 and 1–10–5–7. Choose a position and register that sound clear; shift or use a different string rather than forcing a wide stretch.</li><li><b>Level 7.</b> A compatible scale or note collection. Check the current chord, melody and harmonic setting before choosing it. Play short phrases and resolve tensions; ascending a scale is only one way to learn its sound.</li><li><b>Level 8.</b> Extensions: ninths, elevenths and thirteenths. Begin with one colour against a known chord. They are not equally stable over every voicing: F over Cmaj7, for example, can rub against E. Test register, duration and resolution before combining extensions.</li></ul><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">The horizontal ladder</h3><p>The most valuable drill in the system, run alongside the vertical ladder from the start. Instead of a full arpeggio on each chord, play one degree of every chord across the whole element: all the roots, then all the thirds, then all the fifths, all the sevenths, all the ninths. The player has to find one specific note rather than falling into a shape, and it exposes how little movement there actually is between chords.</p><p>Over Dm7–G7–Cmaj7, the thirds are F–B–E and the sevenths C–F–B. A connected guide-tone line alternates roles: C falls to B, then B stays; another line holds F, then falls to E. A seventh often resolves to the next chord’s third; it does not literally become the same pitch as that third. The two lines contain two semitone moves in total.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Open and closed</h3><p><b>Position-limited.</b> Work in a comfortable four- or five-fret area and choose the nearest useful chord tone; shift if the task requires strain. Position-free. Explore other registers, tenths and string crossings. Both are valuable: the first develops local choices, the second connects the neck. These labels describe the exercise constraint, not standard open- and close-position chord voicings.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Featured drill by stage</h3><p>The whole menu is always available, but each stage puts one drill first, so the menu grows with the material.</p><div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Stage</th><th scope=\"col\">Featured drill</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Home</td><td>Degrees against a drone. Sing, then play, each degree against the home chord and describe its colour in a word or two. Then play a short phrase using one choice for an open degree, and the same phrase with the other choice.</td></tr><tr><td>2. Lean</td><td>The horizontal ladder across the two chords (all roots, all thirds, all fifths, all sevenths), then the moving tones only. In C major the tones that move are E to F and G to A, while C is held.</td></tr><tr><td>3. Pull</td><td>Guide tones across the bond (the third and seventh of each chord), then the leading tone on its own: play only the note that decides whether the bond pulls.</td></tr><tr><td>4. First molecules</td><td>Guide tone chains through each molecule (thirds and sevenths only), then one degree per chord across the whole molecule: all roots, all thirds, all fifths, all sevenths.</td></tr><tr><td>5. Shadow</td><td>Shared tones and moving tones. Play only the notes both chords share, then only the note that moves. Then play upper structures: the same four notes over each root.</td></tr><tr><td>6. Shadow molecules</td><td>Compare parent and substituted progressions on matched accompaniment. Use a track that changes its chords with the exercise, or a sufficiently sparse backing. A bass note alone cannot turn an unchanged F triad into Dm without changing the resulting harmony.</td></tr><tr><td>7. Colour</td><td>The open degree, and the borrowed note. Over the listed triads, compare two compatible choices for the unspecified degree while holding the other notes and rhythm constant. Name the complete collection only after checking all seven notes.</td></tr><tr><td>8. Colour molecules</td><td>Begin with a compatible collection over the diatonic modal loops, then target each chord. For the Andalusian cadence and the major-tonic lift, track the altered notes as the harmony changes rather than imposing one seven-note scale.</td></tr><tr><td>9. Applied</td><td>Target the changed note. Use matched before-and-after accompaniment, then randomise the pair once both versions are familiar. Preserve the groove and register so the harmonic change is the main variable.</td></tr><tr><td>10. Compounds</td><td>Phrasing across the form. Improvise so that phrases start and end with the molecules rather than the bar lines, then deliberately phrase across molecule boundaries.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"method-the-ear-work","title":"9. The ear work","html":"<p>Recognition is discrimination. An element cannot really be recognised until it can be told apart from its neighbours, heard without warning. These tests run in the Test step of every unit.</p><ul><li>Find home. Sing a plausible tonic from a loop, then test another candidate. Say what supports your hearing. Where the loop is genuinely ambiguous, recognising the ambiguity is a valid answer.</li><li>Name the bond. From a randomised quiz track, call out each bond before the loop comes round a second time.</li><li>Sing the moving tone a beat before the change.</li><li>Name the open choice. A line is played with one option for an open degree. Name the mode, or simply the degree (♮6 or ♭6).</li><li>Name the transformation. Before and after versions in random order. Say which is which, and which note changed.</li><li>Call the form. For compounds, name each molecule as it arrives on a first hearing.</li><li>Keep a listening log. One element spotted in a record each week, with a time stamp. This is where the course meets real music.</li></ul>"},{"id":"method-the-rhythmic-axis","title":"10. The rhythmic axis","html":"<p>Every level of the menu can be played at whole notes, halves, quarters, eighths and sixteenths, then displaced, syncopated, with rests and ghost notes. This is where the drilling turns into music. A permutation drill played with real feel is simply a bassline.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Where you put the click</h3><ul><li>All four beats. A useful reference for learning a new rhythm, checking subdivisions and rebuilding steadiness.</li><li>Beats 2 and 4. Hear the click as a backbeat while counting all four beats. To practise at 80 bpm with clicks on 2 and 4, set the metronome to 40 bpm and establish the count before entering.</li><li>Beat 1 only. A whole bar of self supported time between references.</li><li>The offbeat. Disorientating at first, enormously valuable once it settles.</li><li>Beat 2 alone, or beat 4 alone. Holding a reference that is not the downbeat, much closer to what happens in a band.</li><li>Once every two bars, then every four. The honest test.</li><li>On a sixteenth subdivision (the e or the a). Funk specific and directly useful.</li></ul><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">How rhythm and articulation change the style</h3><p>The same progression can support very different music. Take I–IV–V, keep a short pitch sequence identical and vary subdivision, accents, note length, rests and articulation. These changes can transform a bassline’s character. The sketches below are practice contrasts, not definitions of entire genres; use a specific recording as the final reference.</p><div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Feel</th><th scope=\"col\">Subdivision</th><th scope=\"col\">Placement</th><th scope=\"col\">Articulation and density</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Blues, shuffle</td><td>Swung triplet eighths</td><td>Steady pulse; compare the recording</td><td>Try both walking and spacious riffs</td></tr><tr><td>Motown</td><td>Straight eighths</td><td>Try a driving attack without rushing</td><td>Compare melodic movement with simpler parts</td></tr><tr><td>Funk</td><td>Sixteenths</td><td>Locked and centred, hard on the One</td><td>Short and percussive, ghost notes, plenty of space</td></tr><tr><td>Neo soul</td><td>Sixteenths, often swung</td><td>Relaxed placement; keep the pulse stable</td><td>Long notes over a laid back feel, sparse</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>Do not equate relaxed playing with lateness or energetic playing with rushing. Establish a stable pulse, then compare a short recording with the reference track. Harmony and melody still matter: these exercises isolate rhythm so its contribution becomes easier to hear.</p>"},{"id":"method-the-application-step","title":"11. The application step","html":"<p>The last step for every element. Stop drilling and make music from it: write a bassline over it, walk it, solo over it, groove on it, trade phrases, record a take and listen back. Without this step the course becomes another input rather than a synthesis, which is exactly what it is designed to avoid.</p>"},{"id":"method-whose-home-is-it","title":"12. Whose home is it?","html":"<p>A set of chords does not have a home. A band gives it one: by which chord starts the phrase, which chord is held longest, where the drummer’s fill lands, and above all by what the bass does. Am, C, G and D over a bass that keeps returning to A is in A. The same four chords over a bass that keeps returning to G is in G. Nothing in the chords changed.</p><p>That is why some loops in this course have two homes. It is not a flaw in the loop or in your ear. It is the bass player’s decision, which makes it your business more than anyone’s.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">What the research says</h3><p>People who study pop and rock harmony have found the same thing. Mark Richards looked at the four chords of the pop axis in every rotation and concluded that roman numerals struggle with them, because numerals are tied to one centre and the loop has more than one (<a href=\"https://mtosmt.org/ojs/index.php/mto/article/view/308/110\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">“Tonal Ambiguity in Popular Music’s Axis Progressions”</a>, 2017). Mark Spicer describes a “fragile tonic”: the home chord is there, but weakened, for example by sitting in the middle of the phrase and not where phrases start or land (<a href=\"https://www.mtosmt.org/ojs/index.php/mto/article/view/342/112\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">“Fragile, Emergent, and Absent Tonics in Pop and Rock Songs”</a>, 2017). Drew Nobile argues that some of these loops are not one key or the other but both at once, and Trevor de Clercq points out that working musicians in Nashville avoid the choice by numbering a minor home as six (<a href=\"https://mtosmt.org/ojs/index.php/mto/article/view/759\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">“The Logic of Six-Based Minor”</a>, 2021).</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">What the Dojo does about it</h3><ul><li><b>Name it</b> never leaves home in doubt. The band establishes it first: a cue (two bars of the home chord), a pedal (a low home note under the loop), or the band’s bass on the roots. A <b>home hit</b> lays one bar of the home chord over the loop whenever you ask.</li><li><b>Find home</b> gives you nothing. Where a loop has two homes it says so, and either counts. A home has to be one of the chords of the loop: a key whose own chord never sounds is not accepted.</li><li>After any answer, <b>Hear it from each home</b> plays the same chords with the bass on the roots, phrased from each home in turn.</li><li>Cadences (ii V I, the backdoor cadence, the minor cadence and the like) are defined by where they arrive. They have one home.</li></ul><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">What you do about it</h3><p>Take one two homes loop, the Dorian loop or the pop axis. Play a bass line that makes the first home unmistakable: start your phrase on its root, return to it, land on it. Then, without the chords changing, do the same for the other home. When you can move the home at will, you own the loop.</p>"}],"stages":[{"n":1,"id":"stage-1","title":"Stage 1. Home","name":"Home","sub":"One chord, and the notes it leaves open","intro":"<p>Before exploring changes, establish a reference pitch and chord. Hear degrees against that reference, then make short phrases that return convincingly to it. A sustained chord can support a tonal centre, but the listener’s sense of home also depends on what the music does around it.</p><p>A major or minor triad contains three chord tones and leaves four diatonic degree positions unspecified. Start with familiar choices for those positions and compare one note at a time. A melody or accompaniment may already specify them, so listen before treating the vamp as open.</p>","cards":["major-home","dominant-home","minor-home"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Degrees against a drone. Sing, then play, each degree against the home chord and describe its colour in a word or two. Then play a short phrase using one choice for an open degree, and the same phrase with the other choice.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>Sing or match roots, thirds and fifths against a drone in two studied keys; add the seventh and colour-note contrasts as they become secure.</li><li>Hear and name ♮7 against ♭7 over a major-triad backing, and ♮6 against ♭6 over a minor-seventh backing.</li><li>Improvise for a full chorus on a drone using only the degrees the chord fixes, then open one degree at a time.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> One long drone track per home quality (I, I7, i), lightly pulsed, no form. Around five minutes each.</p>"},{"n":2,"id":"stage-2","title":"Stage 2. Lean","name":"Lean","sub":"Bonds with the chord a fifth below home (IV)","intro":"<p>The listed lean triads share one note with home. Their return often has a plagal character, making them useful for practising movement away from and back to a centre. Whether the loop feels settled or expectant depends on the voicing, rhythm and musical context.</p><p>In C–F, F and A add degrees 4 and 6: they are the root and third of IV respectively. D and B remain unspecified by the triads. The minor and blues variants change this inventory, especially when sevenths are present.</p><p>Whose home is it? Every lean bond is also a pull bond heard from the other chair. C to F is I IV in C, but it is also V I in F. Only emphasis, the bass, the melody or a later cadence decides. Hearing that ambiguity is part of the work, not a problem to be solved.</p>","cards":["major-lean","blues-lean","dorian-lean","minor-lean"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> The horizontal ladder across the two chords (all roots, all thirds, all fifths, all sevenths), then the moving tones only. In C major the tones that move are E to F and G to A, while C is held.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>From a randomised track, name each of the four lean bonds within two loops.</li><li>Sing the moving tone of each bond before the change arrives.</li><li>Improvise over C–F triads with B natural, then B♭, and describe the change of colour while keeping rhythm and register constant.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> One loop per bond (two bars per chord) in triads and in sevenths. An ambiguous version of I IV and i7 IV7 with the bass and groove weighted towards the second chord. A Lean quiz track with the four bonds in random eight bar blocks.</p>"},{"n":3,"id":"stage-3","title":"Stage 3. Pull","name":"Pull","sub":"Bonds with the chord a fifth above home (V)","intro":"<p>The pull chord also shares one note with home, but it points somewhere. Its third is the deciding note: a major V contains the leading tone (B in C), which pulls hard towards home, while a minor v has ♭7 instead and floats.</p><p>In C–G triads, D and B add degrees 2 and 7 while F and A remain unspecified. A minor v supplies B♭ instead. Adding G7 supplies F as well. Compare the exact voicings rather than applying the triad inventory to every pull bond.</p><p>This stage ends with a bond between two chords that are not home at all (ii V). It is the pull displaced one step away, and it turns out to be the same two chords as the Dorian lean, heard from a different home.</p>","cards":["major-pull","mixolydian-pull","modal-minor-pull","functional-minor-pull","suspended-cadence"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Guide tones across the bond (the third and seventh of each chord), then the leading tone on its own: play only the note that decides whether the bond pulls.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>Name I V against I v, and i v against i V, by ear from a randomised track.</li><li>Hear i7 IV7 and ii V held open on the same two chords and say which home the track implies.</li><li>Compare a phrase that avoids the absent tonic’s root and third with one that uses them. Then add Cmaj7 after Dm7–G7 and distinguish melodic implication from an actual harmonic arrival.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> One loop per bond in triads and sevenths. A pair track for i7 IV7 and ii V held open on the same chords, with bass and groove placing home differently. A Pull quiz track, and a combined Lean and Pull quiz track.</p>"},{"n":4,"id":"stage-4","title":"Stage 4. First molecules","name":"First molecules","sub":"Lean and pull combined","intro":"<p>I–IV–V combines the lean and pull families. Its three triads contain the complete major-scale collection, making it a useful first full progression. The collection alone does not identify a tonal centre; establish C through the phrase and accompaniment before calling it I–IV–V in C.</p><p>With the diatonic collection established, make the chord changes audible by targeting a chord tone at each arrival. You can still choose colour, register, rhythm and chromatic approaches. The inventory is a map of the harmony, not a list of the only permissible notes.</p><p>The stage ends with the first compound, the twelve bar blues. It appears here because it needs nothing beyond lean, pull and the dominant home.</p>","cards":["parent-molecule","backwards-cadence","modal-minor-molecule","functional-minor-molecule","cadential-cell","cadence-as-a-comma","minor-cadence","twelve-bar-blues-the-first-compound","the-blues-transformation"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Guide tone chains through each molecule (thirds and sevenths only), then one degree per chord across the whole molecule: all roots, all thirds, all fifths, all sevenths.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>Hear ii V I and IV V I back to back and name the difference.</li><li>Play i iv v and i iv V back to back, changing only the one note, in all twelve keys.</li><li>Walk and solo a full twelve bar blues, naming each molecule as it arrives.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> One loop per molecule. A layering track for each (the parent bond for eight bars, then the molecule for eight). A contrast track alternating i iv v and i iv V. The twelve bar blues in shuffle and straight feels.</p>"},{"n":5,"id":"stage-5","title":"Stage 5. Shadow","name":"Shadow","sub":"Bonds with the chords of the key that stand in for home, lean and pull","intro":"<p>The diatonic shadow triads in this stage sit a third from home and share two notes with it. In a close voice-leading arrangement, one voice can move while two remain. Listen for that economy, then check the bass: the change of root can still make a strong expressive difference.</p><p>Because they move so little, shadow bonds fix the fewest degrees, usually leaving three open. The player carries more of the harmony here than anywhere else.</p><p>A useful way to see them: Am7 is C6 with a new root, and Em7 is Cmaj7 with the root replaced by the ninth. The upper notes barely change. The bass tells the story.</p><p>Two shadows sit a step from home rather than a third: ii, which stands in for IV and shares two of its notes, and ♭VII in a minor key, which stands in for v. Their roots move by step against home, but what they do is shadow the lean and the pull.</p>","cards":["relative-shadow","upper-shadow","relative-major","dark-shadow","minor-shift","neo-soul-shift"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Shared tones and moving tones. Play only the notes both chords share, then only the note that moves. Then play upper structures: the same four notes over each root.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>Name the shadow bonds by ear from a randomised track.</li><li>Take a mixed quiz track of all bonds so far (lean, pull and shadow) and name each one.</li><li>Improvise over I vi using only shared tones, then add the moving tone as the target.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> One loop per bond in triads and sevenths. A Shadow quiz track, and a combined quiz across all bonds from Stages 2, 3 and 5.</p>"},{"n":6,"id":"stage-6","title":"Stage 6. Shadow molecules","name":"Shadow molecules","sub":"Substitution arrives, and the pop axis","intro":"<p>With shadows owned, the doo wop, Motown, standards and pop repertoire opens up. The shadows also make the first substitutions possible: ii standing in for IV, vi and iii standing in for I. Each swap changes one note and keeps two.</p><p>The stage ends with I–V–vi–IV, bringing home, pull, shadow and lean into one loop. Reordering or rotating the chords changes the phrase. It may shift the sense of home, but the first chord alone does not determine the tonic.</p>","cards":["fifties-progression","fifties-strengthened","rising-line","surprise-shadow","ascending-soul-cell","long-cycle","pop-axis","axis-minor-view","swap-by-third","the-deceptive-cadence"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Compare the parent and substituted progression over matching accompaniment. First spell the changed chord, then target the changed note while keeping rhythm and register consistent.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>Hear I vi IV V against I vi ii V and name the swapped note.</li><li>Play the pop axis from each of its four starting chords and say where home feels like it is.</li><li>Play guide tones through iii vi ii V I in all twelve keys.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> One loop per molecule. Substitution pair tracks (I vi IV V against I vi ii V, and I IV V against I iii IV V). A rotation track for the pop axis, with the same four chords and the bass entering on a different chord each section.</p>"},{"n":7,"id":"stage-7","title":"Stage 7. Colour","name":"Colour","sub":"Bonds with a chord from outside the key, bringing one note of another mode","intro":"<p>Each colour chord brings one note from outside the key: ♭7 for Mixolydian, ♯4 for Lydian, ♭2 for Phrygian, and ♭6 or ♭3 from the parallel minor. That note is the colour, and it is the one to find and target. Home stays home; the colour chord changes the mode around it.</p><p>The step-related triads among them (♭VII, II and ♭II) share no chord tones with home. Parallel voicings make that contrast especially clear, but parallel motion is an option, not a requirement. Sevenths and extensions may introduce common tones, so count again when the voicings change.</p><p>The borrowed note is always fixed. A mode name describes the complete collection around a tonal centre; one characteristic note is a clue rather than a definition.</p><p>The stage ends with the borrowed minor iv and the chromatic mediants. The mediants' roots sit a third from home, but they share fewer notes with it than the shadows do, and they don't all come from one mode.</p><p>Lydian and Phrygian colours are included as optional cells. They are worth meeting, but less common in the repertoire this course is aimed at.</p>","cards":["mixolydian-shift","lydian-shift","phrygian-shift","borrowed-minor-lean","flat-mediant","major-mediant","flat-submediant"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Over the listed triads, compare choices for the open degree. Then add the expanded voicings and identify which choice the accompaniment now specifies.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>Name the colour bonds by ear, say which note each one brings, and distinguish them from lean, pull and shadow bonds on a mixed quiz track.</li><li>Improvise over i ♭VII choosing ♮6 then ♭6 and say which is Dorian.</li><li>Identify the taught bonds on a mixed quiz, record recurring confusions and retest them in a later session.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> One loop per bond in triads and sevenths. A Colour quiz track, and the full bond quiz across all the relationships.</p>"},{"n":8,"id":"stage-8","title":"Stage 8. Colour molecules","name":"Colour molecules","sub":"Shadows and colours combined","intro":"<p>Combining shadows and colours gives the stepwise, parallel progressions of rock, folk and modal soul. Falling fifths are rare here, so when a real V does appear it is an event.</p><p>The lift and the three molecules after it borrow from the parallel minor: the lift its first two chords, the minor plagal cadence and the Creep progression the minor iv, and the backdoor cadence its iv7 and ♭VII7.</p>","cards":["modal-descent","andalusian-cadence","mixolydian-cadence","lift","minor-plagal-cadence","creep-progression","backdoor-cadence"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Compare a diatonic collection with chord-tone targeting on the modal descent and Mixolydian cadence. On the Andalusian cadence and the major-tonic lift, identify the altered pitches and place them with the chords that introduce them.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>Hear i ♭VII ♭VI ♭VII against i ♭VII ♭VI V and name the moment they diverge.</li><li>Play I ♭VII IV and I IV V back to back and describe the difference in one sentence.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> One loop per molecule. A contrast track alternating i ♭VII ♭VI ♭VII with the Andalusian cadence.</p>"},{"n":9,"id":"stage-9","title":"Stage 9. Applied","name":"Applied","sub":"Pulls aimed at chords other than home","intro":"<p>An applied chord is a pull aimed at a chord other than home: the V of ii, the V of V, the V of vi, the V of IV. It brings one chromatic note, the leading tone of the chord it points at, and for a moment the ear hears that chord as a home. The stage ends with two ways of changing the pull home itself: altering the dominant, and replacing it with the dominant a tritone away.</p><p>The drill pairs each one with its plain version using matching accompaniment. Keep groove and register stable, identify the changed notes and choose one as a melodic target. Do not force a substitute against a backing that still states the original chord unless the combined harmony is the intended exercise.</p><p>The operations that turn one element into another (substitution by third, the deceptive cadence, the parallel swap to sevenths, borrowing, applying) are not a stage of their own. Each sits beside what it makes, and all of them are drilled the same way, with before and after tracks.</p><p>This stage is where the family trees become visible, because the same molecule keeps turning up in a new form.</p>","cards":["turnaround","v-of-v-detour","descending-soul-cycle","chain-of-dominants","v-of-iv","altered-dominant","tritone-substitution"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Target the changed note. Before and after, on the same track, back to back, then in random order until the change can be named by ear.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>From a randomised before and after track, name which transformation has been applied.</li><li>Sing the changed note in each transformation before it arrives.</li><li>Apply a secondary dominant to any molecule from Stages 4 or 6 on request.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> A before and after pair track for each transformation, each also in a randomised order version.</p>"},{"n":10,"id":"stage-10","title":"Stage 10. Compounds","name":"Compounds","sub":"Molecules joined into forms","intro":"<p>Compounds are where the whole course lands. Each form below is built from elements already owned, so the task is recognition in real time: calling out each molecule as it arrives, and choosing notes accordingly.</p><p>The final entries include formal principles as well as chord sequences. Vamp and release contrasts sections; moving home concerns a change of tonal centre. Neither specifies a complete song form on its own.</p>","cards":["quick-change-and-turnaround","minor-blues","jazz-blues","rhythm-changes","major-and-relative-minor-cadences","aaba-song-form","vamp-and-release","key-changes"],"closing":"<p><b>Featured drill.</b> Phrasing across the form. Improvise so that phrases start and end with the molecules rather than the bar lines, then deliberately phrase across molecule boundaries.</p><p><b>Checkpoint.</b> Before moving on, the student can:</p><ul><li>Map a taught compound’s form, then identify familiar molecules on a fresh example. Pause and replay uncertain passages before attempting the real-time version.</li><li>Improvise through each compound in at least two feels.</li><li>Find one compound in a record you did not already know, and map its molecules.</li></ul><p><b>Backing tracks.</b> Each full form in at least two feels. A version of the blues forms with random substitutions from Stage 9, for call the changes practice.</p>"}],"cards":{"major-home":{"id":"major-home","stage":1,"roman":"I","name":"The major home","optional":false,"el":"major-home","rows":[["In C","C"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7"],["Built from","Home alone"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 5 | open: 2, 4, 6, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 3 5 7 | open: 2, 4, 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","Open and settled. Nothing wants to move."],["Note choice","C–E–G supports several collections. Start with D and A natural. Compare F with F♯ while keeping B natural: C Ionian against C Lydian. Then compare B with B♭ while keeping F natural: C Ionian against C Mixolydian. Test one variable at a time; do the B♭ experiment over C triads rather than a backing that insists on B in Cmaj7."],["Listen for","The ♮4 as a note with somewhere to go, the ♯4 as a note that is happy where it is."],["Contrast with","I7 (one note: the seventh)."],["Listening lead","A plain major-triad drone for the first comparison. “Tomorrow Never Knows” is a separate prompt for hearing modal colour around a drone; do not assume its whole texture is an uncoloured C-major triad."]]},"dominant-home":{"id":"dominant-home","stage":1,"roman":"I7","name":"The dominant home","optional":false,"el":"dominant-home","rows":[["In C","C7"],["Expanded voicing","C9"],["Built from","Home alone"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 5 ♭7 | open: 2, 4, 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 5 ♭7 | open: 4, 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","A dominant-seventh chord can feel like home in blues and funk. Its tritone does not oblige the music to resolve elsewhere; function depends on context."],["Note choice","The ♭7 is fixed, so this is Mixolydian territory, with 2, 4 and 6 open. The characteristic move is laying the minor pentatonic over the major chord, so the ♭3 rubs against the chord's 3. That rub is a colour, not a mistake."],["Listen for","The ♭7 heard as colour rather than tension. The ♭3 bending towards the 3."],["Contrast with","I (the ♭7), and i (the third)."],["Listening leads","“Get Up (I Feel Like Being a) Sex Machine”, “Chain of Fools”, one chord blues and funk grooves."]]},"minor-home":{"id":"minor-home","stage":1,"roman":"i","name":"The minor home","optional":false,"el":"minor-home","rows":[["In C","Cm"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7"],["Built from","Home alone"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭3 5 | open: 2, 4, 6, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♭3 5 ♭7 | open: 2, 4, 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","Dark but stable."],["Note choice","Start with C–E♭–G–B♭, then compare A natural with A♭ while keeping D and F natural: C Dorian against C Aeolian. A minor triad alone does not specify B♭; harmonic and melodic minor become useful later. Treat descriptions such as bright or dark as listening prompts rather than fixed emotional meanings."],["Listen for","Sing ♮6 then ♭6 against the drone. Then name which one a record is using."],["Contrast with","I (the parallel major, one note: the third)."],["Listening leads","“Superstition” and “Papa Was a Rollin' Stone” (static minor funk), “So What” (Dorian), Fela Kuti."]]},"major-lean":{"id":"major-lean","stage":2,"roman":"I IV","name":"The major lean","optional":false,"el":"major-lean","rows":[["In C","C F"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Fmaj7"],["Built from","Home, lean"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 4 5 6 | open: 2, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 3 4 5 6 7 | open: 2"]],"paras":[["Sound","Stable and warm. The groove carries the motion because the harmony asks for nothing."],["Note choice","Over C–F triads, begin with D natural and compare B with B♭. B gives C Ionian; B♭ gives C Mixolydian. Against F, the same B is ♯11: a new chord-relative label for the same pitch, not a change of key. Cmaj7–Fmaj7 already specifies B natural."],["Listen for","The semitone from E up to F. It is the tell for this bond."],["Whose home","C F loops equally well as V I in F. Put the weight on F and the bond turns into a pull."],["Contrast with","i IV (one note: the tonic's third). I7 IV7 (the sevenths)."],["Listening leads","“My Girl” (verse), “Walk on the Wild Side”, “In the Midnight Hour”, “Soul Man”."]]},"blues-lean":{"id":"blues-lean","stage":2,"roman":"I7 IV7","name":"The blues lean","optional":false,"el":"blues-lean","rows":[["In C","C7 F7"],["Expanded voicing","C9 F9"],["Built from","Home, lean"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭3 3 4 5 6 ♭7 | open: 2"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 3 4 5 6 ♭7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Gritty and strongly associated with blues. C7 and F7 do not fit one diatonic major scale, but they can sit clearly within a C-centred blues."],["Note choice","The F7 contains E♭, which is the ♭3 of home, so the blue note is part of the harmony rather than a melodic bend. Almost everything is fixed. Only the 2 is left open, and it is nearly always natural."],["Listen for","Two semitone falls happen on the change: E to E♭ and B♭ to A. They are the whole sound of the bond."],["Contrast with","I IV (the same roots with no sevenths)."],["Listening lead","A basic twelve-bar blues often uses I7 and IV7 in bars 1–8; a quick change introduces IV7 in bar 2. Check the selected arrangement because jazz blues and other variants add chords."]]},"dorian-lean":{"id":"dorian-lean","stage":2,"roman":"i7 IV7","name":"The Dorian lean","optional":false,"el":"dorian-lean","rows":[["In C","Cm F"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 F7"],["Built from","Home, lean, colour (IV brings ♮6: Dorian)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭3 4 5 6 | open: 2, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♭3 4 5 6 ♭7 | open: 2"]],"paras":[["Sound","Bright minor. The major IV lifts the minor home. The classic funk and Latin vamp."],["Note choice","The A (third of F) fixes the ♮6, which is exactly what makes this Dorian. Expanded voicing every degree except the 2 is fixed. Over both chords, one set of seven notes works (C Dorian is F Mixolydian), so note choice is about targeting, not changing scale."],["Listen for","The A arriving. It is the brightness."],["Whose home","Cm7–F7 is ii–V if B♭ is heard as the absent destination, and i7–IV7 if C is home. Emphasising F alone does not establish B♭; compare phrase endings or add a B♭ arrival to make that interpretation audible."],["Contrast with","i iv (one note: A against A♭). ii V held open (the same two chords, different home)."],["Listening leads","“Oye Como Va”, “Evil Ways”, “I Wish” (verse), “Chameleon” (heard from B♭), The Meters, Herbie Hancock."]]},"minor-lean":{"id":"minor-lean","stage":2,"roman":"i iv","name":"The minor lean","optional":false,"el":"minor-lean","rows":[["In C","Cm Fm"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 Fm7"],["Built from","Home, lean"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 | open: 2, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: 2"]],"paras":[["Sound","Brooding, heavy, cinematic."],["Note choice","A♭ fixes ♭6. With D, F and B♭, the collection is C Aeolian. With the triads, B is also available as a harmonic-minor colour when the melody and voicing support it; it need not wait for a V chord. Cm7–Fm7 explicitly contains B♭."],["Listen for","G rising a semitone to A♭. The darkening."],["Contrast with","i7 IV7 (A♭ against A)."],["Listening leads","The opening of most minor blues (“The Thrill Is Gone”), minor gospel."]]},"major-pull":{"id":"major-pull","stage":3,"roman":"I V","name":"The major pull","optional":false,"el":"major-pull","rows":[["In C","C G"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 G7"],["Built from","Home, pull"],["Motion","rises a fifth, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 5 7 | open: 4, 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 7 | open: 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","Plain and bright. The V always wants home."],["Note choice","B is fixed, so the leading tone is present. The 4 and 6 are open, and in practice both are natural. With G7 the F arrives and only the 6 stays open."],["Listen for","C falling a semitone to B as the change arrives."],["Whose home","G C looped is IV I in G. Weight the G and it becomes a lean."],["Contrast with","I v (B against B♭)."],["Listening leads","“Iko Iko”, “Jambalaya”, folk and country two step."]]},"mixolydian-pull":{"id":"mixolydian-pull","stage":3,"roman":"I v","name":"The Mixolydian pull","optional":false,"el":"mixolydian-pull","rows":[["In C","C Gm"],["Expanded voicing","C7 Gm7"],["Built from","Home, pull, colour (v brings ♭7: Mixolydian)"],["Motion","rises a fifth, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 5 ♭7 | open: 4, 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 ♭7 | open: 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","The minor v has no leading tone into C, so it often gives a gentler, more modal return. It can still support a clear arrival through bass motion, rhythm and phrasing."],["Note choice","B♭ is fixed, so this is Mixolydian. The 4 and 6 are open and are usually natural."],["Listen for","B♭ where B would be. The missing pull."],["Whose home","Gm7 C7 is ii V in F. This is the jazz blues move in bar four, where v7 I7 leads into IV7."],["Contrast with","I V (B♭ against B)."],["Listening leads","Bar four of a jazz blues, gospel approaches to IV, “Louie Louie” (I IV v IV)."]]},"modal-minor-pull":{"id":"modal-minor-pull","stage":3,"roman":"i v","name":"The modal minor pull","optional":false,"el":"modal-minor-pull","rows":[["In C","Cm Gm"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 Gm7"],["Built from","Home, pull"],["Motion","rises a fifth, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 5 ♭7 | open: 4, 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭7 | open: 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","A modal minor return without the semitone leading tone. It can feel circular or conclusive depending on phrasing."],["Note choice","B♭ is fixed. The 4 and 6 are open: ♭6 gives Aeolian, ♮6 gives Dorian."],["Listen for","The absence of pull, and the B♭ where a B would make it cadence."],["Whose home","As an abstract transposed pair, Dm–Am can suggest D minor or A minor. In the E♭ minor teaching arrangement of “It’s a Man’s Man’s Man’s World”, E♭m–B♭m is i–v; A♭m–B♭7 in the turnaround helps confirm E♭ as home. Hear the whole phrase before deciding."],["Contrast with","i V (B♭ against B)."],["Listening leads","“Ain't No Sunshine” (Withers original), “It's a Man's World” (verse)."]]},"functional-minor-pull":{"id":"functional-minor-pull","stage":3,"roman":"i V","name":"The functional minor pull","optional":false,"el":"functional-minor-pull","rows":[["In C","Cm G7"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 G7"],["Built from","Home, pull"],["Motion","rises a fifth, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 7 | open: 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭7 7 | open: 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","Tense and dramatic. The raised seventh turns a minor colour into a demand."],["Note choice","G7 specifies B and F. C harmonic minor is a useful starting collection over it; C melodic minor gives A natural instead of A♭ where the voicing and melody allow. With Cm7 on the tonic, B♭ belongs to that chord while B belongs to G7. Target the change when using that degree; a line using common tones need not change collection audibly on every chord."],["Listen for","B pulling up to C. Then the same loop with B♭ from the previous bond, back to back."],["Contrast with","i v (one note: B against B♭)."],["Listening leads","“Summertime” (the opening i6 V7 shuttle), tango, flamenco and Balkan dance tunes."]]},"suspended-cadence":{"id":"suspended-cadence","stage":3,"roman":"ii V held open","name":"The suspended cadence","optional":false,"el":"suspended-cadence","rows":[["In C","Dm G"],["Expanded voicing","Dm7 G7"],["Built from","Shadow (ii for IV), pull (home absent)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","2 4 5 6 7 | open: 1, 3"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 4 5 6 7 | open: 3"]],"paras":[["Sound","All approach, no arrival. Tense and relaxed at the same time, which is the engine of a lot of funk and neo soul."],["Note choice","Dm–G supplies D, F, A, G and B, leaving C and E unspecified. Expanded voicing, Dm7 supplies C too and only E remains open. C or E can suggest the absent tonic, but neither guarantees release over G7: C can be an accented 11th against B, and E its 13th. Hear both against the actual voicing, then compare with a real C-major arrival."],["Listen for","The guide tones: C falling to B and F held. The resolution you keep expecting."],["Whose home","Dm7 G7 is also i7 IV7 in D Dorian. Identical chords, different home, and a completely different feeling."],["Contrast with","i7 IV7 (same chords, other home). ii V I (the arrival)."],["Listening leads","Two chord neo soul and funk vamps. “Chameleon” if you hear A♭ as home."]]},"parent-molecule":{"id":"parent-molecule","stage":4,"roman":"I IV V","name":"The parent molecule","optional":false,"el":"parent-molecule","rows":[["In C","C F G"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Fmaj7 G7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a step, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The three functions in their plainest form: home, away and tension."],["Note choice","The three triads supply every degree of C major. Start by targeting their thirds—E on C, A on F and B on G—then connect them with a short phrase. Check each note against its current chord rather than treating every scale tone as equally settled."],["Listen for","IV to V is a step, the only move with no shared notes. Hear the lift before the pull home."],["Contrast with","V IV I (the same chords reversed). I ♭VII IV (the Mixolydian answer in Stage 8)."],["Listening leads","“Twist and Shout”, “Wild Thing”, “La Bamba”."]]},"backwards-cadence":{"id":"backwards-cadence","stage":4,"roman":"V IV I","name":"The backwards cadence","optional":false,"el":"backwards-cadence","rows":[["In C","G F C"],["Expanded voicing","G7 F7 C7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull"],["Motion","falls a step, rises a fifth"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 3 4 5 6 ♭7 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Tension that steps down to IV and then leans home. A shrug rather than a resolution."],["Note choice","In blues sevenths the thirds are B, A and E, and the sevenths are F, E♭ and B♭. Targeting the thirds as they fall is the whole shape of bars nine to eleven."],["Listen for","V to IV is a falling step. Nothing resolves until IV leans home."],["Contrast with","ii V I (the functional route home)."],["Listening leads","Bars nine to eleven of the twelve bar blues, rock choruses."]]},"modal-minor-molecule":{"id":"modal-minor-molecule","stage":4,"roman":"i iv v","name":"The modal minor molecule","optional":false,"el":"modal-minor-molecule","rows":[["In C","Cm Fm Gm"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 Fm7 Gm7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a step, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Dark and circular. It moves without ever arriving."],["Note choice","All seven degrees are fixed as natural minor (Aeolian). One scale covers the molecule."],["Listen for","The v arriving without any pull."],["Contrast with","i iv V: B♭ becomes B in the final triad; adding G7 introduces F as a chord tone too."],["Listening lead","Use the supplied Cm–Fm–Gm loop as the exact model. Compare it with the minor-vamp and major-dominant turnaround in the E♭ minor teaching arrangement of “It’s a Man’s Man’s Man’s World”, whose chord order is different."]]},"functional-minor-molecule":{"id":"functional-minor-molecule","stage":4,"roman":"i iv V","name":"The functional minor molecule","optional":false,"el":"functional-minor-molecule","rows":[["In C","Cm Fm G"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 Fm7 G7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a step, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The same three chords, with one note changed, now cadence."],["Note choice","G major or G7 supplies B, the leading tone of C minor. Cm7 and Fm7 supply B♭. When using the seventh degree, place B on the dominant and B♭ where the minor-seventh voicings call for it. This is ordinary minor-key flexibility; the tonic remains C."],["Listen for","The B. Play this back to back with i iv v in the same key until the difference stops being a concept."],["Contrast with","i iv v."],["Listening lead","Minor blues endings and other i–iv–V phrases. The course’s E♭ minor arrangement of “It’s a Man’s Man’s Man’s World” combines i–v with iv–V7; use it as a comparison, not an exact i–iv–V transcription."]]},"cadential-cell":{"id":"cadential-cell","stage":4,"roman":"ii V I","name":"The cadential cell","optional":false,"el":"cadential-cell","rows":[["In C","Dm G C"],["Expanded voicing","Dm7 G7 Cmaj7"],["Built from","Home, pull, shadow (ii for IV)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, falls a fifth"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Approach, tension, home, in three chords. The most drilled molecule in music."],["Note choice","Thirds are F–B–E; sevenths are C–F–B. Connect them as F–F–E and C–B–B. Each line has one semitone move and one repeated pitch: two semitone moves in total. Hear the held note as clearly as the moving one."],["Listen for","Two falling fifths in a row. Then the guide tones on their own."],["Whose home","ii is IV with one note changed (D replaces C in the F chord), so this is IV V I with a substitution. Stage 9 names that operation."],["Contrast with","IV V I. iiø7 V7 i. ii V held open."],["Listening leads","“Autumn Leaves”, “Satin Doll”, everywhere from Motown to neo soul."]]},"cadence-as-a-comma":{"id":"cadence-as-a-comma","stage":4,"roman":"ii V I IV","name":"The cadence as a comma","optional":false,"el":"cadence-as-a-comma","rows":[["In C","Dm G C F"],["Expanded voicing","Dm7 G7 Cmaj7 Fmaj7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull, shadow (ii for IV)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, falls a fifth, falls a fifth, falls a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","It resolves and then keeps moving, so the cadence becomes a comma rather than a full stop."],["Note choice","The E of Cmaj7 is held into Fmaj7 as its seventh. Holding that note across the change is a strong melodic choice."],["Listen for","The lean after the arrival, and the way it sets up the next ii."],["Contrast with","ii V I."],["Listening leads","“Autumn Leaves” (opening), gospel and soul turnarounds."]]},"minor-cadence":{"id":"minor-cadence","stage":4,"roman":"iiø7 V7 i","name":"The minor cadence","optional":false,"el":"minor-cadence","rows":[["In C","Dø7 G7 Cm"],["Expanded voicing","Dø7 G7 Cm7"],["Built from","Home, pull, shadow (iiø for iv)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, falls a fifth"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The same shape as ii V I, darker in both the approach and the arrival."],["Note choice","C harmonic minor contains every chord tone of Dø7 and G7. It is one available collection, not a requirement to emphasise B over Dø7. Resolve to C–E♭–G; if the tonic is Cm7, use B♭ for its seventh. Cm6 and CmMaj7 require different tonic choices."],["Listen for","A♭ in the approach chord, B in the dominant, E♭ in the arrival."],["Contrast with","Dm7–G7–Cmaj7. Dm7 becomes Dø7 by lowering A to A♭; G7 stays unchanged; Cmaj7 becomes Cm7 by lowering E to E♭ and B to B♭."],["Listening leads","“Autumn Leaves” (second half), “Blue Bossa”, “Sunny”, “Ain't No Sunshine” (jazz arrangement)."]]},"twelve-bar-blues-the-first-compound":{"id":"twelve-bar-blues-the-first-compound","stage":4,"roman":"Twelve bar blues","name":"The first compound","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Form","<span class=\"he-form\"><i>I7</i><i>IV7</i><i>I7</i><i>I7</i><i>IV7</i><i>IV7</i><i>I7</i><i>I7</i><i>V7</i><i>IV7</i><i>I7</i><i>V7</i></span>"],["In C","<span class=\"he-form\"><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>C7</i><i>G7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>G7</i></span>"],["Built from","I7 IV7 (bars one to eight), V IV I (bars nine to eleven), a V7 turnaround"],["Notes in play","All seven of C major, plus ♭3 and ♭7"]],"paras":[["Sound","The first compound: a lean bond, a backwards cadence and a turnaround joined into a twelve bar form."],["Note choice","The ♭3 (E♭) is available throughout as colour. The targets on the changes are the thirds: E on C7, A on F7, B on G7. Moving between ♭3 as colour and 3 as target is the essential blues vocabulary."],["Listen for","Where each molecule begins. Call out the bond, the backwards cadence and the turnaround as they arrive."],["Contrast with","The jazz blues in Stage 10."],["Listening leads","“Johnny B. Goode”, “Pride and Joy”, “Green Onions”, “Papa's Got a Brand New Bag”."]]},"the-blues-transformation":{"id":"the-blues-transformation","stage":4,"roman":"Parallel swap: I IV V becomes I7 IV7 V7","name":"The blues transformation","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Before","I IV V (C F G)"],["After","I7 IV7 V7 (C7 F7 G7)"],["What changes","B♭, E♭ and F added"]],"paras":[["Sound","Dominant-seventh colour added to the three primary chords. Rhythm, melody and phrasing are still needed to turn the progression into a convincing blues."],["Note choice","Add B♭ to C, E♭ to F and F to G. The harmony now mixes natural and lowered degrees around C; it has not lost its tonal centre. Explore E♭ as a blue colour against C7’s E, while targeting each chord’s defining tones."],["Listen for","The ♭7s arriving one chord at a time."],["Contrast with","The original, back to back."],["Listening leads","Any rock and roll song played as a blues."]]},"relative-shadow":{"id":"relative-shadow","stage":5,"roman":"I vi","name":"The relative shadow","optional":false,"el":"relative-shadow","rows":[["In C","C Am"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Am7"],["Built from","Home, shadow (vi for I)"],["Motion","falls a third, rises a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 5 6 | open: 2, 4, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 3 5 6 7 | open: 2, 4"]],"paras":[["Sound","Smooth voice leading with two common triad tones. A cloud passing over the sun."],["Note choice","For C–Am, hold C and E while G moves to A. With Cmaj7–Am7, C, E and G can stay while B moves to A. The shared tones give a simple line across both chords; other notes still need testing against the melody and voicing."],["Listen for","The root falling a third while the upper notes stay put."],["Whose home","C Am looped can settle in A minor, where it becomes ♭III i."],["Contrast with","i ♭III (the same shape from a minor home)."],["Listening leads","“What's Going On” (opening), doo wop introductions."]]},"upper-shadow":{"id":"upper-shadow","stage":5,"roman":"I iii","name":"The upper shadow","optional":false,"el":"upper-shadow","rows":[["In C","C Em"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Em7"],["Built from","Home, shadow (iii for I)"],["Motion","rises a third, falls a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 5 7 | open: 2, 4, 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 5 7 | open: 4, 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","Slightly wistful. The iii is the home chord with its root swapped for the seventh."],["Note choice","With triads C moves to B, and the 2, 4 and 6 stay open. Expanded voicing the D arrives and the 4 and 6 stay open. The 4 (F) sits awkwardly over Em, so the practical choice is usually around it."],["Listen for","C falling to B while E and G stay."],["Contrast with","I vi (the other shadow). I V (C to B also happens there, but E moves too)."],["Listening leads","Neo soul and dream pop shuttles (worth collecting examples from your own listening), and as a stand in for I in I iii IV V."]]},"relative-major":{"id":"relative-major","stage":5,"roman":"i ♭III","name":"The relative major","optional":false,"el":"relative-major","rows":[["In C","Cm E♭"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 E♭maj7"],["Built from","Home, shadow (♭III for i)"],["Motion","rises a third, falls a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭3 5 ♭7 | open: 2, 4, 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 5 ♭7 | open: 4, 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","Minor brightening into its relative major. Hopeful."],["Note choice","With triads C moves to B♭, and the 2, 4 and 6 stay open. Expanded voicing D arrives and the 4 and 6 stay open, leaving the Dorian or Aeolian decision to the player."],["Listen for","The lift into major with only one note moving."],["Contrast with","I vi (the major home version of the same relationship)."],["Listening leads","“Mad World” (the opening move)."]]},"dark-shadow":{"id":"dark-shadow","stage":5,"roman":"i ♭VI","name":"The dark shadow","optional":false,"el":"dark-shadow","rows":[["In C","Cm A♭"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 A♭maj7"],["Built from","Home, shadow (♭VI for iv)"],["Motion","falls a third, rises a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭3 5 ♭6 | open: 2, 4, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♭3 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: 2, 4"]],"paras":[["Sound","Sinking and cinematic."],["Note choice","A♭ specifies ♭6. With triads, G can rise a semitone to A♭ while C and E♭ stay. D, F and B♭ complete C Aeolian, but the triads themselves leave 2, 4 and 7 unspecified."],["Listen for","G rising a semitone to A♭, the same darkening as in i iv but with the bass falling instead."],["Contrast with","i iv (both fix A♭; different roots)."],["Listening leads","“Eleanor Rigby” (verse), “Zombie” (opening), film scores."]]},"minor-shift":{"id":"minor-shift","stage":5,"roman":"i ♭VII","name":"The minor step","optional":false,"el":"minor-shift","rows":[["In C","Cm B♭"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 B♭"],["Built from","Home, shadow (♭VII for v)"],["Motion","falls a step, rises a step (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭7 | open: 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭7 | open: 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","Minor folk rock. Drifting rather than going anywhere."],["Note choice","Only the 6 is open. ♮6 makes it Dorian, ♭6 makes it Aeolian, and that single choice changes the mood of everything."],["Listen for","The parallel step, and then whichever 6 the melody chooses."],["Contrast with","I ♭VII."],["Listening lead","Begin with Cm–B♭ as an exact teaching loop. Add a verified minor-rock example from the student’s listening log; check inversions and added notes before reducing it to two triads."]]},"neo-soul-shift":{"id":"neo-soul-shift","stage":5,"roman":"I ii","name":"The neo soul step","optional":false,"el":"neo-soul-shift","rows":[["In C","C Dm"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Dm7"],["Built from","Home, shadow (ii for IV)"],["Motion","rises a step, falls a step (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 | open: 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Gentle and sophisticated."],["Note choice","With triads only the 7 is open. Expanded voicing nothing is. Because ii shares two notes with IV, this is also a lean bond in disguise."],["Listen for","The root rising a step with the texture moving in parallel."],["Whose home","Dm7 Cmaj7 heard from D is i ♭VII in D Dorian."],["Contrast with","I IV (one note: D replaces C in the second chord). I II (F against F♯)."],["Listening leads","Neo soul and bossa shuttles (worth collecting examples from your own listening)."]]},"fifties-progression":{"id":"fifties-progression","stage":6,"roman":"I vi IV V","name":"The fifties progression","optional":false,"el":"fifties-progression","rows":[["In C","C Am F G"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Am7 Fmaj7 G7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull, shadow (vi for I)"],["Motion","falls a third, falls a third, rises a step, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The relative minor gives major key music its first shadow, then lean, lift and pull."],["Note choice","vi and IV share two notes (A and C), so the first half barely moves. The only step is IV to V, where nothing is shared."],["Listen for","Two falling thirds, one step, one falling fifth."],["Contrast with","I vi ii V replaces IV with ii. I V vi IV reorders the same four chords."],["Listening leads","“Stand By Me”, “Earth Angel”, “Every Breath You Take”."]]},"fifties-strengthened":{"id":"fifties-strengthened","stage":6,"roman":"I vi ii V","name":"The fifties, strengthened","optional":false,"el":"fifties-strengthened","rows":[["In C","C Am Dm G"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Am7 Dm7 G7"],["Built from","Home, pull, shadow (vi for I, ii for IV)"],["Motion","falls a third, falls a fifth, falls a fifth, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The same shape with a stronger pull. After the first shadow, every root falls a fifth."],["Note choice","D replaces C in the third chord. Guide tones now chain through three falling fifths."],["Listen for","The D in the bass where F was."],["Contrast with","I vi IV V."],["Listening leads","“Blue Moon”, “Heart and Soul”, the A section of rhythm changes."]]},"rising-line":{"id":"rising-line","stage":6,"roman":"I iii IV V","name":"The rising line","optional":false,"el":"rising-line","rows":[["In C","C Em F G"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Em7 Fmaj7 G7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull, shadow (iii for I)"],["Motion","rises a third, rises a semitone, rises a step, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Ascending and lifting. The iii is a second home chord with a different bass note."],["Note choice","The bass climbs by third then step then step. A stepwise ascending line in the melody against it is the obvious choice."],["Listen for","The bass rising C, E, F, G."],["Contrast with","I IV V (the parent). I IV iii V."],["Listening leads","Sixties pop and Motown ballads (collect examples and check against the record)."]]},"surprise-shadow":{"id":"surprise-shadow","stage":6,"roman":"I IV iii V","name":"The surprise shadow","optional":false,"el":"surprise-shadow","rows":[["In C","C F Em G"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Fmaj7 Em7 G7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull, shadow (iii for I)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, falls a semitone, rises a third, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The same ingredients reordered, so the iii arrives as a surprise rather than a step."],["Note choice","IV to iii is a falling step with nothing shared. The E in the bass is the moment to target."],["Listen for","The bass stepping down from F to E."],["Contrast with","I iii IV V."],["Listening leads","Sixties pop (collect examples and check against the record)."]]},"ascending-soul-cell":{"id":"ascending-soul-cell","stage":6,"roman":"IV V iii vi","name":"The ascending soul cell","optional":false,"el":"ascending-soul-cell","rows":[["In C","F G Em Am"],["Expanded voicing","Fmaj7 G7 Em7 Am7"],["Built from","Lean, pull, shadow (iii, vi) (home absent)"],["Motion","rises a step, falls a third, falls a fifth, falls a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","A rise through IV–V, then movement through iii into vi. Depending on the phrase, the relative minor may feel like the destination."],["Note choice","The C-major triad is absent, although C itself appears in F and Am. Target the actual chords rather than assuming the tonic note is missing. If A is heard as home, the same sequence is ♭VI–♭VII–v–i."],["Listen for","V moving to iii before vi. It avoids a direct V–I arrival; distinguish this from the standard deceptive V–vi cadence."],["Contrast with","ii V held open (the other molecule that avoids home)."],["Listening leads","Soul and later pop. Known in Japanese pop as the royal road progression."]]},"long-cycle":{"id":"long-cycle","stage":6,"roman":"iii vi ii V I","name":"The long cycle","optional":false,"el":"long-cycle","rows":[["In C","Em Am Dm G C"],["Expanded voicing","Em7 Am7 Dm7 G7 Cmaj7"],["Built from","Home, pull, shadow (iii, vi, ii)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, falls a fifth, falls a fifth, falls a fifth"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Four falling fifths in a row. Momentum."],["Note choice","Track the sevenths toward the next thirds: D–C, G–F, C–B and F–E. The first two moves are whole tones and the last two semitones. Practise two connected guide-tone lines rather than treating these separate pairs as one continuous line."],["Listen for","Each root falling a fifth, and the sense of the chain gathering speed."],["Contrast with","ii V I. III7 VI7 II7 V7 I (the same chain with secondary dominants, Stage 9)."],["Listening leads","Stevie Wonder, the cycle passages in “Fly Me to the Moon” and “All the Things You Are”."]]},"pop-axis":{"id":"pop-axis","stage":6,"roman":"I V vi IV","name":"The pop axis","optional":false,"el":"pop-axis","rows":[["In C","C G Am F"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 G Am7 Fmaj7"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull, shadow (vi for I)"],["Motion","rises a fifth, rises a step, falls a third, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Bright, open, endlessly reusable."],["Note choice","Home, pull, shadow and lean all appear, so all seven degrees are fixed. The top line C, B, C, C (or E, D, C, C) sits over everything."],["Listen for","V moving up a step to vi, where the fifties progression would have gone home."],["Contrast with","I vi IV V: the same four chords in a different order."],["Listening leads","“Let It Be”, “No Woman, No Cry”, “With or Without You”, “Don't Stop Believin'”."]]},"axis-minor-view":{"id":"axis-minor-view","stage":6,"roman":"vi IV I V","name":"The axis, minor view","optional":false,"el":"axis-minor-view","rows":[["In C","Am F C G"],["Expanded voicing","Am7 Fmaj7 Cmaj7 G"],["Built from","Home, lean, pull, shadow (vi for I)"],["Motion","falls a third, rises a fifth, rises a fifth, rises a step (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The same four chords starting on vi. The ear may take A minor as home, and the loop turns melancholy."],["Note choice","Nothing changes in the notes. Everything changes in which note feels like home, so the phrasing decides: land on A and it is minor, land on C and it is major."],["Listen for","Where the loop seems to begin, and which chord you feel as home."],["Whose home","Heard in A minor this is i ♭VI ♭III ♭VII."],["Contrast with","I V vi IV."],["Listening leads","Countless modern pop choruses."]]},"swap-by-third":{"id":"swap-by-third","stage":6,"roman":"Substitution: IV becomes ii","name":"Swap by third","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Before","IV V I (F G C)"],["After","ii V I (Dm G C)"],["What changes","C becomes D"]],"paras":[["Sound","Same function, stronger root motion."],["Note choice","IV and ii share F and A. Swapping them changes one note and turns a step into a falling fifth. The same swap turns I vi IV V into I vi ii V."],["Listen for","The bass note, and the stronger pull that follows."],["Contrast with","Its reverse (ii becomes IV), heard back to back."],["Listening leads","Everywhere. Most jazz reharmonisation starts here."]]},"the-deceptive-cadence":{"id":"the-deceptive-cadence","stage":6,"roman":"Substitution: I becomes vi","name":"The deceptive cadence","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Before","IV V I (F G C)"],["After","IV V vi (F G Am)"],["What changes","G becomes A"]],"paras":[["Sound","Arrival denied, softly."],["Note choice","vi shares C and E with I. The ear gets two of the three notes it expected."],["Listen for","The bass rising a step where it should have fallen a fifth."],["Contrast with","IV V I."],["Listening leads","Soul ballads, hymns, pop bridges."]]},"mixolydian-shift":{"id":"mixolydian-shift","stage":7,"roman":"I ♭VII","name":"The Mixolydian step","optional":false,"el":"mixolydian-shift","rows":[["In C","C B♭"],["Expanded voicing","C B♭maj7"],["Built from","Home, colour (♭VII brings ♭7: Mixolydian)"],["Motion","falls a step, rises a step (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 ♭7 | open: 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 6 ♭7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Rock swagger and jam band ease. Home without any leading tone."],["Note choice","Six degrees are fixed, including ♭7. Only the 6 is open, and it is almost always natural (Mixolydian)."],["Listen for","Every note falling a step at once."],["Contrast with","I v (both fix B♭). i ♭VII (the tonic's third)."],["Listening leads","“Fire on the Mountain”, Mixolydian rock vamps."]]},"lydian-shift":{"id":"lydian-shift","stage":7,"roman":"I II","name":"The Lydian step","optional":true,"el":"lydian-shift","rows":[["In C","C D"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 D7"],["Built from","Home, colour (II brings ♯4: Lydian)"],["Motion","rises a step, falls a step (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 ♯4 5 6 | open: 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 ♯4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Bright, floating, dreamy."],["Note choice","C–D fixes F♯. Add B natural for C Lydian; B♭ gives C Lydian dominant. Cmaj7–D7 fixes B natural, so the expanded version supports Lydian. In a song, D7 may instead function as V7/V; hear where the phrase goes."],["Listen for","F♯ in the second chord, heard against the home that stays major."],["Contrast with","I ii."],["Listening lead","Compare the I–II7 opening colour in “The Girl from Ipanema” or “Take the A Train” with a sustained C–D vamp. Their later harmony matters: an opening II7 is not proof that the whole passage is modal Lydian."]]},"phrygian-shift":{"id":"phrygian-shift","stage":7,"roman":"i ♭II","name":"The Phrygian step","optional":true,"el":"phrygian-shift","rows":[["In C","Cm D♭"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 D♭maj7"],["Built from","Home, colour (♭II brings ♭2: Phrygian)"],["Motion","rises a semitone, falls a semitone (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 | open: 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♭2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Dark, Spanish, menacing."],["Note choice","Cm–D♭ fixes ♭2 and ♭6. Add B♭ for C Phrygian; the triads alone do not settle degree 7. With C major as home, C–D♭ plus B♭ supports C Phrygian dominant. A B natural instead gives a different collection, so do not infer the full mode from the two triads alone."],["Listen for","The semitone rise in the bass."],["Contrast with","i ♭VII (the whole step down against the half step up)."],["Listening lead","Compare the supplied minor-tonic and major-tonic ♭II loops. Flamenco and “Misirlou” are prompts for examining different Phrygian-related collections; check their tonic quality and seventh rather than treating them as interchangeable examples."]]},"borrowed-minor-lean":{"id":"borrowed-minor-lean","stage":7,"roman":"I iv","name":"The borrowed minor lean","optional":false,"el":"borrowed-minor-lean","rows":[["In C","C Fm"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 Fm7"],["Colour","Borrowing"],["Built from","Home, lean, colour (iv brings ♭6, from minor)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 4 5 ♭6 | open: 2, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♭3 3 4 5 ♭6 7 | open: 2"]],"paras":[["Sound","Perhaps the most emotionally loaded single move in soul. Bittersweet."],["Note choice","A♭ is the borrowed note, fixed as ♭6. The 2 and 7 stay open."],["Listen for","A♭ in Fm and its semitone return to G in C. Compare with F major, where the corresponding chord tone is A."],["Contrast with","I IV (one note)."],["Listening leads","Gospel amen cadences, soul ballads."]]},"flat-mediant":{"id":"flat-mediant","stage":7,"roman":"I ♭III","name":"The flat mediant","optional":false,"el":"flat-mediant","rows":[["In C","C E♭"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 E♭maj7"],["Colour","Chromatic mediant"],["Built from","Home, colour (♭III, a chromatic mediant)"],["Motion","rises a third, falls a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭3 3 5 ♭7 | open: 2, 4, 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 3 5 ♭7 7 | open: 4, 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","A sideways shift into another world. Cinematic."],["Note choice","E♭ and B♭ arrive against the home chord's E and B. G is the only shared note."],["Listen for","G held while everything around it changes colour."],["Contrast with","I iii (the diatonic shadow)."],["Listening leads","Film music, neo soul."]]},"major-mediant":{"id":"major-mediant","stage":7,"roman":"I III","name":"The major mediant","optional":false,"el":"major-mediant","rows":[["In C","C E"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 E7"],["Colour","Chromatic mediant"],["Built from","Home, colour (III, a chromatic mediant)"],["Motion","rises a third, falls a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 5 ♯5 7 | open: 2, 4, 6"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 5 ♯5 7 | open: 4, 6"]],"paras":[["Sound","A bright, uplifting jolt."],["Note choice","G♯ is the changed note. E is shared."],["Listen for","G rising a semitone to G♯."],["Contrast with","I iii (G against G♯)."],["Listening leads","“Creep” (opening move), film music."]]},"flat-submediant":{"id":"flat-submediant","stage":7,"roman":"I ♭VI","name":"The flat submediant","optional":false,"el":"flat-submediant","rows":[["In C","C A♭"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 A♭maj7"],["Colour","Chromatic mediant"],["Built from","Home, colour (♭VI, a chromatic mediant)"],["Motion","falls a third, rises a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♭3 3 5 ♭6 | open: 2, 4, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♭3 3 5 ♭6 7 | open: 2, 4"]],"paras":[["Sound","A dramatic, darkening sidestep."],["Note choice","A♭ and E♭ arrive. C is the shared note, and it is the anchor for a melody."],["Listen for","C held while the harmony sinks beneath it."],["Contrast with","I vi (A against A♭)."],["Listening leads","Film music, rock ballads."]]},"modal-descent":{"id":"modal-descent","stage":8,"roman":"i ♭VII ♭VI ♭VII","name":"The modal descent","optional":false,"el":"modal-descent","rows":[["In C","Cm B♭ A♭ B♭"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 B♭ A♭maj7 B♭"],["Built from","Home, shadow (♭VII for v, ♭VI for iv)"],["Motion","falls a step, falls a step, rises a step, rises a step (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Falls, then turns back on itself. Circular and unresolved."],["Note choice","All seven degrees are fixed as natural minor. The line can sit on one scale the whole way round."],["Listen for","The bass turning back up where a cadence might have been."],["Contrast with","i ♭VII ♭VI V."],["Listening leads","“All Along the Watchtower”, rock and funk vamps."]]},"andalusian-cadence":{"id":"andalusian-cadence","stage":8,"roman":"i ♭VII ♭VI V","name":"The Andalusian cadence","optional":false,"el":"andalusian-cadence","rows":[["In C","Cm B♭ A♭ G"],["Expanded voicing","Cm7 B♭ A♭maj7 G7"],["Built from","Home, shadow (♭VII, ♭VI), pull"],["Motion","falls a step, falls a step, falls a semitone, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","A falling stepwise bass with a real dominant waiting at the bottom."],["Note choice","B♭ on the ♭VII, B on the V. The same letter name changes within one loop, and the line has to follow it."],["Listen for","The moment B♭ becomes B."],["Contrast with","i ♭VII ♭VI ♭VII: the last B♭ triad (B♭–D–F) is replaced by G (G–B–D), retaining D. This is a chord substitution, not a one-note alteration of the same triad."],["Listening leads","“Hit the Road Jack”, “Runaway”, flamenco."]]},"mixolydian-cadence":{"id":"mixolydian-cadence","stage":8,"roman":"I ♭VII IV","name":"The Mixolydian cadence","optional":false,"el":"mixolydian-cadence","rows":[["In C","C B♭ F"],["Built from","Home, colour (♭VII), lean"],["Motion","falls a step, rises a fifth, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed, open","1 2 3 4 5 6 ♭7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Rock resolution without a dominant. Home is reached by leaning, not pulling."],["Note choice","C–B♭–F supplies the C Mixolydian collection. Make each chord audible with targets and phrasing; B♭ distinguishes the collection from C major, but it does not by itself determine the genre."],["Listen for","The IV to I lean where you might expect V to I."],["Contrast with","I IV V."],["Listening leads","“Sweet Home Alabama”, “Hey Jude” (coda), “Gloria”."]]},"lift":{"id":"lift","stage":8,"roman":"♭VI ♭VII I","name":"The lift","optional":false,"el":"lift","rows":[["In C","A♭ B♭ C"],["Expanded voicing","A♭maj7 B♭ C"],["Built from","Colour (♭VI, ♭VII, from minor), home"],["Motion","rises a step, rises a step"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 ♭3 3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","A triumphant rise into a major home."],["Note choice","The first two chords come from C minor (A♭, E♭, B♭), then E natural arrives with the home chord. The E is the payoff note."],["Listen for","The E arriving on the last chord."],["Contrast with","IV V I (the diatonic lift)."],["Listening leads","Anthemic rock and gospel endings."]]},"minor-plagal-cadence":{"id":"minor-plagal-cadence","stage":8,"roman":"IV iv I","name":"The minor plagal cadence","optional":false,"el":"minor-plagal-cadence","rows":[["In C","F Fm C"],["Expanded voicing","Fmaj7 Fm7 Cmaj7"],["Colour","Borrowing"],["Built from","Home, lean, colour (iv brings ♭6, from minor)"],["Motion","holds, rises a fifth"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 4 5 ♭6 6 | open: 2, 7"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♭3 3 4 5 ♭6 6 7 | open: 2"]],"paras":[["Sound","The amen cadence with a tear in it."],["Note choice","A to A♭ to G is a descending chromatic line that carries the whole molecule."],["Listen for","The falling chromatic inner voice."],["Contrast with","IV I."],["Listening leads","“In My Life”, gospel endings."]]},"creep-progression":{"id":"creep-progression","stage":8,"roman":"I III IV iv","name":"The Creep progression","optional":false,"el":"creep-progression","rows":[["In C","C E F Fm"],["Colour","Borrowing"],["Built from","Home, lean, colour (III brings ♯5, iv brings ♭6)"],["Motion","rises a third, rises a semitone, holds, rises a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed, open","1 3 4 5 ♯5 ♭6 6 7 | open: 2"]],"paras":[["Sound","Yearning, then collapsing."],["Note choice","G♯ appears in the III and A♭ in the iv: the same pitch, spelled and heard two ways within one loop."],["Listen for","The major III lifting, the minor iv sinking."],["Contrast with","I iii IV (the diatonic version of the first move)."],["Listening leads","“Creep”."]]},"backdoor-cadence":{"id":"backdoor-cadence","stage":8,"roman":"iv7 ♭VII7 I","name":"The backdoor cadence","optional":false,"el":"backdoor-cadence","rows":[["In C","Fm B♭7 C"],["Expanded voicing","Fm7 B♭7 Cmaj7"],["Colour","Borrowing"],["Built from","Home, colour (iv7 and ♭VII7, from minor)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, rises a step"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 ♭3 3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Arriving home through the back door. Warm, knowing."],["Note choice","A♭ and B♭ are borrowed. They resolve to G and C (or to B, the major seventh of the home chord)."],["Listen for","Home arriving without the leading tone B ever sounding first."],["Contrast with","ii V I."],["Listening leads","“Just Friends”, Steely Dan, gospel."]]},"turnaround":{"id":"turnaround","stage":9,"roman":"I VI7 ii V","name":"The turnaround","optional":false,"el":"turnaround","rows":[["In C","C A7 Dm G7"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 A7 Dm7 G7"],["Applied","Secondary dominant"],["Built from","Home, applied (VI7, the V of ii), shadow (ii), pull"],["Motion","falls a third, falls a fifth, falls a fifth, falls a fifth (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♯1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♯1 2 3 4 5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The fifties progression given a push. The A7 points at Dm."],["Note choice","C♯ is the leading tone into Dm. D harmonic minor over A7 gives A Phrygian dominant, a useful first option with B♭ as ♭9. It is not the only option: check the melody and extensions, and hear how C♯ resolves to D."],["Listen for","C♯, and the pull into ii that it creates."],["Contrast with","I vi ii V."],["Listening leads","Jazz blues bars eleven and twelve, rhythm changes turnarounds, doo wop endings."]]},"v-of-v-detour":{"id":"v-of-v-detour","stage":9,"roman":"I II7 IV I","name":"The V of V detour","optional":false,"el":"v-of-v-detour","rows":[["In C","C D7 F C"],["Applied","Secondary dominant"],["Built from","Home, applied (II7, the V of V), lean"],["Motion","rises a step, rises a third, rises a fifth"],["Fixed, open","1 2 3 4 ♯4 5 6 | open: 7"]],"paras":[["Sound","Bright and cheeky. The D7 points at G and goes to F instead."],["Note choice","F♯ is the changed note, and it drops to F on the next chord. Target that semitone."],["Listen for","F♯ to F."],["Contrast with","I ii IV I, then I II7 V I."],["Listening leads","“Eight Days a Week”."]]},"descending-soul-cycle":{"id":"descending-soul-cycle","stage":9,"roman":"IV III7 vi","name":"The descending soul cycle","optional":false,"el":"descending-soul-cycle","rows":[["In C","F E7 Am"],["Expanded voicing","Fmaj7 E7 Am7"],["Applied","Secondary dominant"],["Built from","Lean, applied (III7, the V of vi), shadow (vi)"],["Motion","falls a semitone, falls a fifth, falls a third (as a loop)"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 2 3 4 ♯5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 2 3 4 5 ♯5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","Silky and descending. The E7 pulls into the relative minor."],["Note choice","G♯ is the changed note, leading into A. Over E7, A harmonic minor (E Phrygian dominant) covers it."],["Listen for","G♯ resolving up to A."],["Contrast with","IV iii vi."],["Listening leads","“Just the Two of Us” and its many descendants."]]},"chain-of-dominants":{"id":"chain-of-dominants","stage":9,"roman":"III7 VI7 II7 V7 I","name":"The chain of dominants","optional":false,"el":"chain-of-dominants","rows":[["In C","E7 A7 D7 G7 C"],["Expanded voicing","E7 A7 D7 G7 Cmaj7"],["Applied","Secondary dominant"],["Built from","Home, applied (III7, VI7 and II7, each the V of the next), pull"],["Motion","falls a fifth, falls a fifth, falls a fifth, falls a fifth"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 ♯1 2 3 4 ♯4 5 ♯5 6 7 | open: None"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 ♯1 2 3 4 ♯4 5 ♯5 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","The long cycle, turbocharged."],["Note choice","At successive changes, sevenths resolve to thirds: D→C♯, G→F♯, C→B, then F→E into C. These are voice-leading pairs, not one continuous chromatic melody. Connect them through alternating third/seventh roles and keep track of which chord is sounding."],["Listen for","The falling semitone guide tone line."],["Contrast with","iii vi ii V I."],["Listening leads","The bridge of rhythm changes, “Sweet Georgia Brown”."]]},"v-of-iv":{"id":"v-of-iv","stage":9,"roman":"I I7 IV","name":"The V of IV","optional":false,"el":"v-of-iv","rows":[["In C","C C7 F"],["Expanded voicing","Cmaj7 C7 Fmaj7"],["Applied","Secondary dominant"],["Built from","Home, applied (I7, the V of IV), lean"],["Motion","holds, falls a fifth"],["Fixed and open in base voicing","1 3 4 5 6 ♭7 | open: 2"],["Fixed and open in expanded voicing","1 3 4 5 6 ♭7 7 | open: 2"]],"paras":[["Sound","Home turning into a signpost."],["Note choice","B becomes B♭. That single semitone turns the tonic into the dominant of IV."],["Listen for","B falling to B♭ before the lean."],["Contrast with","I IV."],["Listening leads","Rhythm changes bar five, gospel."]]},"altered-dominant":{"id":"altered-dominant","stage":9,"roman":"ii V7alt I","name":"The altered dominant","optional":false,"el":"altered-dominant","rows":[["In C","Dm7 G7alt Cmaj7"],["Applied","Dominant alteration"],["Built from","Home, shadow (ii), pull (altered)"],["Motion","falls a fifth, falls a fifth"],["Fixed","G7alt fixes G, B and F; choose altered ninths and fifths to match the voicing"]],"paras":[["Sound","Maximum tension before arrival."],["Note choice","A common G altered collection is G–A♭–B♭–B–D♭–E♭–F. The chord symbol fixes the root, major third and minor seventh; it does not require every possible alteration at once. Try A♭→G, B♭→B, D♭→C and E♭→E into Cmaj7. Choose one or two tensions and make their destinations audible."],["Listen for","A♭ resolving down to G and E♭ resolving up to E. E♭→D is also a semitone, but D is the tonic’s ninth, not a tone of Cmaj7."],["Contrast with","ii V I."],["Listening leads","Jazz standards, jazz inflected soul."]]},"tritone-substitution":{"id":"tritone-substitution","stage":9,"roman":"ii ♭II7 I","name":"The tritone substitution","optional":false,"el":"tritone-substitution","rows":[["In C","Dm7 D♭7 Cmaj7"],["Applied","Dominant alteration"],["Built from","Home, shadow (ii), applied (♭II7 for V)"],["Motion","falls a semitone, falls a semitone"],["Fixed, open","1 ♭2 2 3 4 5 ♭6 6 7 | open: None"]],"paras":[["Sound","A slinky chromatic slide home."],["Note choice","D♭7 shares the tritone F–C♭ with G7’s F–B, using an enharmonic spelling. That gives related dominant voice leading while the bass moves D–D♭–C. The root, upper colours and melodic context change, so the effect is related rather than identical."],["Listen for","The bass sliding D, D♭, C."],["Contrast with","ii V I."],["Listening lead","Compare Dm7–G7–Cmaj7 with Dm7–D♭7–Cmaj7 on a controlled loop, then find a verified tritone substitution in a selected jazz arrangement."]]},"quick-change-and-turnaround":{"id":"quick-change-and-turnaround","stage":10,"roman":"Twelve bar blues, revisited","name":"Quick change and turnaround","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Form","<span class=\"he-form\"><i>I7</i><i>IV7</i><i>I7</i><i>I7</i><i>IV7</i><i>IV7</i><i>I7</i><i>VI7</i><i>ii7</i><i>V7</i><i>I7 VI7</i><i>ii7 V7</i></span>"],["In C","<span class=\"he-form\"><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>A7</i><i>Dm7</i><i>G7</i><i>C7 A7</i><i>Dm7 G7</i></span>"],["Built from","The Stage 4 blues, plus the turnaround (I VI7 ii V) and ii V in place of the backwards cadence"],["Notes in play","All seven of C major, plus ♯1, ♭3 and ♭7"]],"paras":[["Sound","The blues moving towards jazz without losing its grit."],["Note choice","Bars nine and ten become a ii V, so guide tones replace the blues shrug. Bar eight's A7 points at Dm7."],["Listen for","Bar eight, and whether bars nine and ten are V IV or ii V."],["Contrast with","The Stage 4 twelve bar blues."],["Listening leads","Soul jazz, blues bands with jazz players."]]},"minor-blues":{"id":"minor-blues","stage":10,"roman":"","name":"Minor blues","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Form","<span class=\"he-form\"><i>i7</i><i>i7</i><i>i7</i><i>i7</i><i>iv7</i><i>iv7</i><i>i7</i><i>i7</i><i>♭VI7</i><i>V7</i><i>i7</i><i>V7</i></span>"],["In C","<span class=\"he-form\"><i>Cm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>Fm7</i><i>Fm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>A♭7</i><i>G7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>G7</i></span>"],["Built from","i iv (bars one to eight), a stepwise approach from ♭VI7 into V7, and the functional minor pull"],["Notes in play","All seven of C minor, plus ♭5 and ♮7"]],"paras":[["Sound","Dark and intense."],["Note choice","C Aeolian is a starting point over Cm7–Fm7. C Dorian’s A natural would conflict with the A♭ specified by Fm7, so it is not a blanket scale for both chords. A♭7 introduces G♭; G7 introduces B natural. Target those local chord tones and return to the tonic collection as the harmony resolves."],["Listen for","The semitone slide from A♭7 to G7."],["Contrast with","Twelve bar blues."],["Listening leads","“Mr. P.C.”, “The Thrill Is Gone” (a variant)."]]},"jazz-blues":{"id":"jazz-blues","stage":10,"roman":"","name":"Jazz blues","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Form","<span class=\"he-form\"><i>I7</i><i>IV7</i><i>I7</i><i>v7 I7</i><i>IV7</i><i>IV7</i><i>I7</i><i>VI7</i><i>ii7</i><i>V7</i><i>I7 VI7</i><i>ii7 V7</i></span>"],["In C","<span class=\"he-form\"><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>Gm7 C7</i><i>F7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>A7</i><i>Dm7</i><i>G7</i><i>C7 A7</i><i>Dm7 G7</i></span>"],["Built from","The revisited blues plus v7 I7 (ii V into IV) in bar four"],["Notes in play","All seven of C major, plus ♯1, ♭3 and ♭7"]],"paras":[["Sound","The blues as a jazz vehicle."],["Note choice","Bar four is the Mixolydian pull (I v) heard as ii V of IV. Guide tones run through bars eight to twelve without a break."],["Listen for","Bar four and bar eight, the two new signposts."],["Contrast with","Twelve bar blues, revisited."],["Listening leads","“Now's the Time”, “Tenor Madness”, “Billie's Bounce”."]]},"rhythm-changes":{"id":"rhythm-changes","stage":10,"roman":"","name":"Rhythm changes","optional":false,"el":"rhythm-changes","rows":[["Form","A1/A2/A3, eight bars each: | I vi | ii V | I vi | ii V | I I7 | IV iv | I V | I V |. B, eight bars: | III7 | III7 | VI7 | VI7 | II7 | II7 | V7 | V7 |. Play AABA; end the final A on I."],["In C","A: | C Am | Dm G7 | C Am | Dm G7 | C C7 | F Fm | C G7 | C G7 |. B: | E7 | E7 | A7 | A7 | D7 | D7 | G7 | G7 |. Two chords in a bar receive two beats each in this 4/4 study. Use C for the final bar of the last A."],["Built from","I vi ii V, the V of IV, the borrowed iv, and the chain of dominants as the bridge (AABA)"],["Notes in play","All seven of C major, plus ♯1, ♯4, ♯5, ♭6 and ♭7"]],"paras":[["Sound","Fast, bright and full of motion."],["Note choice","This is a simplified study arrangement of rhythm changes, not a definitive chart for every tune. The A section combines tonic motion, ii–V and a borrowed iv. The bridge has one dominant per two bars; connect each seventh to the next third by semitone, then resolve into the returning A section."],["Listen for","The bridge arriving, and the Fm in bar six."],["Contrast with","I vi ii V."],["Listening leads","“I Got Rhythm”, “Oleo”, “Anthropology”."]]},"major-and-relative-minor-cadences":{"id":"major-and-relative-minor-cadences","stage":10,"roman":"Two homes with one altered note","name":"Major and relative minor cadences","optional":false,"el":"major-and-relative-minor-cadences","rows":[["Form","ii V I IV | iiø7 V7 i"],["In C","Dm7 G7 Cmaj7 Fmaj7 | Bø7 E7 Am"],["Built from","ii V I IV, then iiø7 V7 i in the relative minor"],["Notes in play","All seven of C major, plus ♯5"]],"paras":[["Sound","Home moving to its own shadow and back."],["Note choice","Most of this progression uses the shared notes of C major and A natural minor. E7 adds G♯, so it cannot all be described by one unchanged seven-note scale. Hear G♯ leading to A and decide whether A has become a local arrival or a sustained new tonic."],["Listen for","The E7, and the moment the ear accepts A as home."],["Contrast with","ii V I and iiø7 V7 i on their own."],["Listening leads","“Autumn Leaves”, “Fly Me to the Moon”."]]},"aaba-song-form":{"id":"aaba-song-form","stage":10,"roman":"","name":"AABA song form","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Form","A common design: three eight-bar A sections and one contrasting eight-bar B section, ordered AABA. The bridge may change key, harmony, melody or texture; a new key is not required."],["In C","Varies by tune"],["Built from","Repeated A material and a contrasting B; the harmonic contents depend on the tune"]],"paras":[["Sound","Statement, repetition, departure, return."],["Note choice","Map each section's home before playing. In the bridge, identify which chord has become temporary home, then use the Stage 9 tools to leave it."],["Listen for","The last two bars of the second A, where the bridge is prepared."],["Contrast with","Rhythm changes (a specific AABA)."],["Listening leads","“Blue Moon”, “Satin Doll”, “Take the A Train”."]]},"vamp-and-release":{"id":"vamp-and-release","stage":10,"roman":"","name":"Vamp and release","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Form","A static or two chord vamp (tension and space) that releases into a cadential section"],["In C","Varies by tune"],["Built from","Home or a bond for the vamp, a molecule for the release"]],"paras":[["Sound","Tension held, then let go."],["Note choice","Two sets of rules in one tune: modal colour choices in the vamp, chord tone targeting in the release. The skill is switching instantly."],["Listen for","The bar before the release, and how the band signals it."],["Contrast with","Any bond on its own against the full piece."],["Listening leads","“Superstition” (vamp against chorus), “Chameleon”, much funk and Afrobeat arrangement."]]},"key-changes":{"id":"key-changes","stage":10,"roman":"Moving home","name":"Key changes","optional":false,"el":null,"rows":[["Form","A new tonic established by a phrase or cadence, or a direct shift to a new key. A brief applied dominant may tonicise a chord without changing the prevailing key."],["In C","From C: A♭7 → D♭ to establish D♭ major, or A7 → D to establish D major. A direct C → D♭ or C → D shift is also possible; G7 normally leads to C."],["Built from","Familiar chord relationships reinterpreted around a new tonic; often prepared by its dominant"]],"paras":[["Sound","Lift, surprise, renewal."],["Note choice","Every element in this course is portable. After a key change, rename everything relative to the new home and carry on."],["Listen for","The pivot chord, and the first moment the new home feels settled."],["Contrast with","A secondary dominant that returns (I VI7 ii V)."],["Listening leads","“Love on Top” (repeated lifts), “Man in the Mirror”."]]}},"reference":[{"id":"ref-overview","title":"Overview reference table","html":"<p>Every element in teaching order. Kind is Home (one chord), Bond (two chords and their relationship), Molecule (a short progression built from bonds), Compound (molecules joined into a form) or Transformation (an operation that changes one or two notes of something already owned). Built from names what each chord does in the key: home, lean (the IV side), pull (the V side), shadow (a chord of the key that stands in for one of those three), colour (a chord from outside the key, bringing one note of another mode) or applied (a pull aimed at a chord other than home).</p><div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Kind</th><th scope=\"col\">Element</th><th scope=\"col\">In C</th><th scope=\"col\">Built from</th><th scope=\"col\">Chord inventory</th><th scope=\"col\">Open</th><th scope=\"col\">What it teaches</th><th scope=\"col\">Listening leads</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 1. Home: One chord, and the notes it leaves open</th></tr><tr><td>Home</td><td>I (The major home)</td><td>C</td><td>Home alone</td><td>1 3 5</td><td>2, 4, 6, 7</td><td>The reference point. Open degrees heard as colour: ♮4 or ♯4, ♮7 or ♭7.</td><td>A plain major-triad drone for the first comparison. “Tomorrow Never Knows” is a separate prompt for hearing modal colour around a drone; do not assume its whole texture is an uncoloured C-major triad.</td></tr><tr><td>Home</td><td>I7 (The dominant home)</td><td>C7</td><td>Home alone</td><td>1 3 5 ♭7</td><td>2, 4, 6</td><td>A dominant chord that does not need to resolve. The blue ♭3 against the chord's 3.</td><td>“Get Up (I Feel Like Being a) Sex Machine”, “Chain of Fools”, one chord blues and funk grooves.</td></tr><tr><td>Home</td><td>i (The minor home)</td><td>Cm</td><td>Home alone</td><td>1 ♭3 5</td><td>2, 4, 6, 7</td><td>With 2, 4 and ♭7, compare ♮6 Dorian against ♭6 Aeolian.</td><td>“Superstition” and “Papa Was a Rollin' Stone” (static minor funk), “So What” (Dorian), Fela Kuti.</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 2. Lean: Bonds with the chord a fifth below home (IV)</th></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I IV (The major lean)</td><td>C F</td><td>Home, lean</td><td>1 3 4 5 6</td><td>2, 7</td><td>A useful plagal loop. 2 and 7 open: Ionian or Mixolydian.</td><td>“My Girl” (verse), “Walk on the Wild Side”, “In the Midnight Hour”, “Soul Man”.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I7 IV7 (The blues lean)</td><td>C7 F7</td><td>Home, lean</td><td>1 ♭3 3 4 5 6 ♭7</td><td>2</td><td>Two dominant sevenths outside one diatonic major collection. The blue ♭3 written into the harmony.</td><td>A basic twelve-bar blues often uses I7 and IV7 in bars 1–8; a quick change introduces IV7 in bar 2. Check the selected arrangement because jazz blues and other variants add chords.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>i7 IV7 (The Dorian lean)</td><td>Cm F</td><td>Home, lean, colour (IV brings ♮6: Dorian)</td><td>1 ♭3 4 5 6</td><td>2, 7</td><td>Minor home with a major lean. The fixed ♮6 is the Dorian signature.</td><td>“Oye Como Va”, “Evil Ways”, “I Wish” (verse), “Chameleon” (heard from B♭), The Meters, Herbie Hancock.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>i iv (The minor lean)</td><td>Cm Fm</td><td>Home, lean</td><td>1 ♭3 4 5 ♭6</td><td>2, 7</td><td>Minor loop with ♭6; add 2 and ♭7 for Aeolian.</td><td>The opening of most minor blues (“The Thrill Is Gone”), minor gospel.</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 3. Pull: Bonds with the chord a fifth above home (V)</th></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I V (The major pull)</td><td>C G</td><td>Home, pull</td><td>1 2 3 5 7</td><td>4, 6</td><td>Plain functional pull. 7 fixed, 4 and 6 open.</td><td>“Iko Iko”, “Jambalaya”, folk and country two step.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I v (The Mixolydian pull)</td><td>C Gm</td><td>Home, pull, colour (v brings ♭7: Mixolydian)</td><td>1 2 3 5 ♭7</td><td>4, 6</td><td>A pull with no leading tone. ♭7 fixed: Mixolydian.</td><td>Bar four of a jazz blues, gospel approaches to IV, “Louie Louie” (I IV v IV).</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>i v (The modal minor pull)</td><td>Cm Gm</td><td>Home, pull</td><td>1 2 ♭3 5 ♭7</td><td>4, 6</td><td>Minor v has no leading tone; its return depends on phrasing. 4 and 6 open.</td><td>“Ain't No Sunshine” (Withers original), “It's a Man's World” (verse).</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>i V (The functional minor pull)</td><td>Cm G7</td><td>Home, pull</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 7</td><td>6</td><td>The raised seventh. Tension held open. Harmonic or melodic minor.</td><td>“Summertime” (the opening i6 V7 shuttle), tango, flamenco and Balkan dance tunes.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>ii V held open (The suspended cadence)</td><td>Dm G</td><td>Shadow (ii for IV), pull (home absent)</td><td>2 4 5 6 7</td><td>1, 3</td><td>The cadence that never lands. Home notes are the open ones.</td><td>Two chord neo soul and funk vamps. “Chameleon” if you hear A♭ as home.</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 4. First molecules: Lean and pull combined</th></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I IV V (The parent molecule)</td><td>C F G</td><td>Home, lean, pull</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Three triads supply the major collection; phrasing establishes home.</td><td>“Twist and Shout”, “Wild Thing”, “La Bamba”.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>V IV I (The backwards cadence)</td><td>G F C</td><td>Home, lean, pull</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>V moves to IV before a plagal return; common in blues.</td><td>Bars nine to eleven of the twelve bar blues, rock choruses.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>i iv v (The modal minor molecule)</td><td>Cm Fm Gm</td><td>Home, lean, pull</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>Natural minor fully declared. Circular, with no arrival.</td><td>Use the supplied Cm–Fm–Gm loop as the exact model. Compare it with the minor-vamp and major-dominant turnaround in the E♭ minor teaching arrangement of “It’s a Man’s Man’s Man’s World”, whose chord order is different.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>i iv V (The functional minor molecule)</td><td>Cm Fm G</td><td>Home, lean, pull</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Same three chords, one note changed. The raised seventh turns a colour into a cadence.</td><td>Minor blues endings and other i–iv–V phrases. The course’s E♭ minor arrangement of “It’s a Man’s Man’s Man’s World” combines i–v with iv–V7; use it as a comparison, not an exact i–iv–V transcription.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>ii V I (The cadential cell)</td><td>Dm G C</td><td>Home, pull, shadow (ii for IV)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Guide-tone lines F–F–E and C–B–B connect thirds and sevenths.</td><td>“Autumn Leaves”, “Satin Doll”, everywhere from Motown to neo soul.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>ii V I IV (The cadence as a comma)</td><td>Dm G C F</td><td>Home, lean, pull, shadow (ii for IV)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Resolves, then leans away. A comma rather than a full stop.</td><td>“Autumn Leaves” (opening), gospel and soul turnarounds.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>iiø7 V7 i (The minor cadence)</td><td>Dø7 G7 Cm</td><td>Home, pull, shadow (iiø for iv)</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 7</td><td>None</td><td>The minor cadence. One scale (harmonic minor) covers the approach.</td><td>“Autumn Leaves” (second half), “Blue Bossa”, “Sunny”, “Ain't No Sunshine” (jazz arrangement).</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Twelve bar blues (The first compound)</td><td><span class=\"he-form\"><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>C7</i><i>G7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>G7</i></span></td><td>I7 IV7 (bars one to eight), V IV I (bars nine to eleven), a V7 turnaround</td><td>All seven of C major, plus ♭3 and ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>Bonds and molecules joined into a form. The tonic’s blue ♭3 alongside local chord-tone targets.</td><td>“Johnny B. Goode”, “Pride and Joy”, “Green Onions”, “Papa's Got a Brand New Bag”.</td></tr><tr><td>Transformation</td><td>Parallel swap: I IV V becomes I7 IV7 V7 (The blues transformation)</td><td>C F G → C7 F7 G7</td><td>I IV V → I7 IV7 V7</td><td>Changes: B♭, E♭ and F added</td><td></td><td>Dominant sevenths add blues colour without erasing the tonal centre.</td><td>Any rock and roll song played as a blues.</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 5. Shadow: Chords of the key that stand in for home, lean and pull</th></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I vi (The relative shadow)</td><td>C Am</td><td>Home, shadow (vi for I)</td><td>1 3 5 6</td><td>2, 4, 7</td><td>Two shared triad tones and one moving voice.</td><td>“What's Going On” (opening), doo wop introductions.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I iii (The upper shadow)</td><td>C Em</td><td>Home, shadow (iii for I)</td><td>1 3 5 7</td><td>2, 4, 6</td><td>iii as I with its root replaced. Wistful.</td><td>Neo soul and dream pop shuttles (worth collecting examples from your own listening), and as a stand in for I in I iii IV V.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>i ♭III (The relative major)</td><td>Cm E♭</td><td>Home, shadow (♭III for i)</td><td>1 ♭3 5 ♭7</td><td>2, 4, 6</td><td>Minor brightening into its relative major.</td><td>“Mad World” (the opening move).</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>i ♭VI (The dark shadow)</td><td>Cm A♭</td><td>Home, shadow (♭VI for iv)</td><td>1 ♭3 5 ♭6</td><td>2, 4, 7</td><td>♭6 fixed; Aeolian is one compatible collection.</td><td>“Eleanor Rigby” (verse), “Zombie” (opening), film scores.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>i ♭VII (The minor step)</td><td>Cm B♭</td><td>Home, shadow (♭VII for v)</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭7</td><td>6</td><td>One open note decides Dorian or Aeolian.</td><td>Begin with Cm–B♭ as an exact teaching loop. Add a verified minor-rock example from the student’s listening log; check inversions and added notes before reducing it to two triads.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I ii (The neo soul step)</td><td>C Dm</td><td>Home, shadow (ii for IV)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6</td><td>7</td><td>A lean in disguise: ii is IV's substitute. Only the 7 open.</td><td>Neo soul and bossa shuttles (worth collecting examples from your own listening).</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 6. Shadow molecules: Substitution arrives, and the pop axis</th></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I vi IV V (The fifties progression)</td><td>C Am F G</td><td>Home, lean, pull, shadow (vi for I)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Home, shadow, lean, pull. The relative minor enters major key music.</td><td>“Stand By Me”, “Earth Angel”, “Every Breath You Take”.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I vi ii V (The fifties, strengthened)</td><td>C Am Dm G</td><td>Home, pull, shadow (vi for I, ii for IV)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>IV swapped for ii. Roots now fall in fifths, so the pull strengthens.</td><td>“Blue Moon”, “Heart and Soul”, the A section of rhythm changes.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I iii IV V (The rising line)</td><td>C Em F G</td><td>Home, lean, pull, shadow (iii for I)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>iii standing in for a second I. Ascending.</td><td>Sixties pop and Motown ballads (collect examples and check against the record).</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I IV iii V (The surprise shadow)</td><td>C F Em G</td><td>Home, lean, pull, shadow (iii for I)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Same ingredients reordered, so the iii arrives as a surprise.</td><td>Sixties pop (collect examples and check against the record).</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>IV V iii vi (The ascending soul cell)</td><td>F G Em Am</td><td>Lean, pull, shadow (iii, vi) (home absent)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Moves through IV–V–iii–vi; I is absent, though its root is present in other chords.</td><td>Soul and later pop. Known in Japanese pop as the royal road progression.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>iii vi ii V I (The long cycle)</td><td>Em Am Dm G C</td><td>Home, pull, shadow (iii, vi, ii)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Four falling fifths in a row. The pull bond chained.</td><td>Stevie Wonder, the cycle passages in “Fly Me to the Moon” and “All the Things You Are”.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I V vi IV (The pop axis)</td><td>C G Am F</td><td>Home, lean, pull, shadow (vi for I)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>Home, pull, shadow and lean in one loop. The fifties progression’s chords reordered.</td><td>“Let It Be”, “No Woman, No Cry”, “With or Without You”, “Don't Stop Believin'”.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>vi IV I V (The axis, minor view)</td><td>Am F C G</td><td>Home, lean, pull, shadow (vi for I)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>The same four chords from vi. Home becomes ambiguous.</td><td>Countless modern pop choruses.</td></tr><tr><td>Transformation</td><td>Substitution: IV becomes ii (Swap by third)</td><td>F G C → Dm G C</td><td>IV V I → ii V I</td><td>Changes: C becomes D</td><td></td><td>These diatonic triads share two notes; compare their different roots and functions.</td><td>Everywhere. Most jazz reharmonisation starts here.</td></tr><tr><td>Transformation</td><td>Substitution: I becomes vi (The deceptive cadence)</td><td>F G C → F G Am</td><td>IV V I → IV V vi</td><td>Changes: G becomes A</td><td></td><td>The arrival replaced by its shadow.</td><td>Soul ballads, hymns, pop bridges.</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 7. Colour: One note from outside the key</th></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I ♭VII (The Mixolydian step)</td><td>C B♭</td><td>Home, colour (♭VII brings ♭7: Mixolydian)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 ♭7</td><td>6</td><td>Home without a leading tone. Only the 6 open.</td><td>“Fire on the Mountain”, Mixolydian rock vamps.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I II (optional) (The Lydian step)</td><td>C D</td><td>Home, colour (II brings ♯4: Lydian)</td><td>1 2 3 ♯4 5 6</td><td>7</td><td>♯4 fixed. Bright and floating.</td><td>Compare the I–II7 opening colour in “The Girl from Ipanema” or “Take the A Train” with a sustained C–D vamp. Their later harmony matters: an opening II7 is not proof that the whole passage is modal Lydian.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>i ♭II (optional) (The Phrygian step)</td><td>Cm D♭</td><td>Home, colour (♭II brings ♭2: Phrygian)</td><td>1 ♭2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6</td><td>7</td><td>♭2 fixed. Dark and Spanish.</td><td>Compare the supplied minor-tonic and major-tonic ♭II loops. Flamenco and “Misirlou” are prompts for examining different Phrygian-related collections; check their tonic quality and seventh rather than treating them as interchangeable examples.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I iv (The borrowed minor lean)</td><td>C Fm</td><td>Home, lean, colour (iv brings ♭6, from minor)</td><td>1 3 4 5 ♭6</td><td>2, 7</td><td>The minor iv imported into a major key. A♭ enters.</td><td>Gospel amen cadences, soul ballads.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I ♭III (The flat mediant)</td><td>C E♭</td><td>Home, colour (♭III, a chromatic mediant)</td><td>1 ♭3 3 5 ♭7</td><td>2, 4, 6</td><td>Roots a third apart without shared key. Sideways, cinematic.</td><td>Film music, neo soul.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I III (The major mediant)</td><td>C E</td><td>Home, colour (III, a chromatic mediant)</td><td>1 3 5 ♯5 7</td><td>2, 4, 6</td><td>A bright chromatic lift. G♯ enters.</td><td>“Creep” (opening move), film music.</td></tr><tr><td>Bond</td><td>I ♭VI (The flat submediant)</td><td>C A♭</td><td>Home, colour (♭VI, a chromatic mediant)</td><td>1 ♭3 3 5 ♭6</td><td>2, 4, 7</td><td>Dark shadow borrowed into major. C shared.</td><td>Film music, rock ballads.</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 8. Colour molecules: Shadows and colours combined</th></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>i ♭VII ♭VI ♭VII (The modal descent)</td><td>Cm B♭ A♭ B♭</td><td>Home, shadow (♭VII for v, ♭VI for iv)</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>A falling line that turns back on itself instead of cadencing.</td><td>“All Along the Watchtower”, rock and funk vamps.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>i ♭VII ♭VI V (The Andalusian cadence)</td><td>Cm B♭ A♭ G</td><td>Home, shadow (♭VII, ♭VI), pull</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7 7</td><td>None</td><td>A stepwise falling bass with a real dominant waiting at the bottom.</td><td>“Hit the Road Jack”, “Runaway”, flamenco.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I ♭VII IV (The Mixolydian cadence)</td><td>C B♭ F</td><td>Home, colour (♭VII), lean</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>Home reached by leaning rather than pulling.</td><td>“Sweet Home Alabama”, “Hey Jude” (coda), “Gloria”.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>♭VI ♭VII I (The lift)</td><td>A♭ B♭ C</td><td>Colour (♭VI, ♭VII, from minor), home</td><td>1 2 ♭3 3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>A triumphant rise, borrowed from minor, into a major home.</td><td>Anthemic rock and gospel endings.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>IV iv I (The minor plagal cadence)</td><td>F Fm C</td><td>Home, lean, colour (iv brings ♭6, from minor)</td><td>1 3 4 5 ♭6 6</td><td>2, 7</td><td>A falls to A♭, then to G. The amen with a tear in it.</td><td>“In My Life”, gospel endings.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I III IV iv (The Creep progression)</td><td>C E F Fm</td><td>Home, lean, colour (III brings ♯5, iv brings ♭6)</td><td>1 3 4 5 ♯5 ♭6 6 7</td><td>2</td><td>A chromatic mediant and a borrowed iv in one loop.</td><td>“Creep”.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>iv7 ♭VII7 I (The backdoor cadence)</td><td>Fm B♭7 C</td><td>Home, colour (iv7 and ♭VII7, from minor)</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>Arriving home from below, without the dominant.</td><td>“Just Friends”, Steely Dan, gospel.</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 9. Applied: Pulls aimed at chords other than home</th></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I VI7 ii V (The turnaround)</td><td>C A7 Dm G7</td><td>Home, applied (VI7, the V of ii), shadow (ii), pull</td><td>1 ♯1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>vi made dominant to pull into ii. The first chromatic note: C♯.</td><td>Jazz blues bars eleven and twelve, rhythm changes turnarounds, doo wop endings.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I II7 IV I (The V of V detour)</td><td>C D7 F C</td><td>Home, applied (II7, the V of V), lean</td><td>1 2 3 4 ♯4 5 6</td><td>7</td><td>A secondary dominant that does not go where it points.</td><td>“Eight Days a Week”.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>IV III7 vi (The descending soul cycle)</td><td>F E7 Am</td><td>Lean, applied (III7, the V of vi), shadow (vi)</td><td>1 2 3 4 ♯5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>III7 pulling into vi. G♯ enters.</td><td>“Just the Two of Us” and its many descendants.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>III7 VI7 II7 V7 I (The chain of dominants)</td><td>E7 A7 D7 G7 C</td><td>Home, applied (III7, VI7 and II7, each the V of the next), pull</td><td>1 ♯1 2 3 4 ♯4 5 ♯5 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>The long cycle with dominant-seventh approach chords. Guide tones fall by semitone.</td><td>The bridge of rhythm changes, “Sweet Georgia Brown”.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>I I7 IV (The V of IV)</td><td>C C7 F</td><td>Home, applied (I7, the V of IV), lean</td><td>1 3 4 5 6 ♭7</td><td>2</td><td>C gains B♭ to become C7; from Cmaj7, B falls to B♭.</td><td>Rhythm changes bar five, gospel.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>ii V7alt I (The altered dominant)</td><td>Dm7 G7alt Cmaj7</td><td>Home, shadow (ii), pull (altered)</td><td>G7alt: G, B, F plus chosen altered ninths and fifths</td><td>Depends on voicing</td><td>Maximum tension on the dominant. Every altered tension resolves by step.</td><td>Jazz standards, jazz inflected soul.</td></tr><tr><td>Molecule</td><td>ii ♭II7 I (The tritone substitution)</td><td>Dm7 D♭7 Cmaj7</td><td>Home, shadow (ii), applied (♭II7 for V)</td><td>1 ♭2 2 3 4 5 ♭6 6 7</td><td>None</td><td>The V replaced by the dominant a tritone away. The bass falls by semitone.</td><td>Compare Dm7–G7–Cmaj7 with Dm7–D♭7–Cmaj7 on a controlled loop, then find a verified tritone substitution in a selected jazz arrangement.</td></tr><tr class=\"is-group\"><th colspan=\"8\" scope=\"colgroup\">Stage 10. Compounds: Molecules joined into forms</th></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Twelve bar blues, revisited (Quick change and turnaround)</td><td><span class=\"he-form\"><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>A7</i><i>Dm7</i><i>G7</i><i>C7 A7</i><i>Dm7 G7</i></span></td><td>The Stage 4 blues, plus the turnaround (I VI7 ii V) and ii V in place of the backwards cadence</td><td>All seven of C major, plus ♯1, ♭3 and ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>The first compound, upgraded with substitution and secondary dominants.</td><td>Soul jazz, blues bands with jazz players.</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Minor blues</td><td><span class=\"he-form\"><i>Cm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>Fm7</i><i>Fm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>A♭7</i><i>G7</i><i>Cm7</i><i>G7</i></span></td><td>i iv (bars one to eight), a stepwise approach from ♭VI7 into V7, and the functional minor pull</td><td>All seven of C minor, plus ♭5 and ♮7</td><td>None</td><td>The minor lean and pull in blues form. ♭VI7 slides into V7.</td><td>“Mr. P.C.”, “The Thrill Is Gone” (a variant).</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Jazz blues</td><td><span class=\"he-form\"><i>C7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>Gm7 C7</i><i>F7</i><i>F7</i><i>C7</i><i>A7</i><i>Dm7</i><i>G7</i><i>C7 A7</i><i>Dm7 G7</i></span></td><td>The revisited blues plus v7 I7 (ii V into IV) in bar four</td><td>All seven of C major, plus ♯1, ♭3 and ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>Every Stage 9 operation that suits the blues, applied at once.</td><td>“Now's the Time”, “Tenor Madness”, “Billie's Bounce”.</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Rhythm changes</td><td>A: | C Am | Dm G7 | C Am | Dm G7 | C C7 | F Fm | C G7 | C G7 |. B: E7, A7, D7, G7 for two bars each. AABA; final A ends on C.</td><td>I vi ii V, the V of IV, the borrowed iv, and the chain of dominants as the bridge (AABA)</td><td>All seven of C major, plus ♯1, ♯4, ♯5, ♭6 and ♭7</td><td>None</td><td>Shadow molecules, borrowing and the chain of dominants joined into AABA.</td><td>“I Got Rhythm”, “Oleo”, “Anthropology”.</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Two homes with one altered note (Major cadence and relative minor cadence)</td><td>Dm7 G7 Cmaj7 Fmaj7 | Bø7 E7 Am</td><td>ii V I IV in C, then iiø7 V7 i in the relative minor (A)</td><td>All seven of C major, plus ♯5</td><td>None</td><td>C major and A minor share a key signature; E7 adds G♯.</td><td>“Autumn Leaves”, “Fly Me to the Moon”.</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>AABA song form</td><td>Varies by tune</td><td>Repeated A material and a contrasting B; the harmonic contents depend on the tune</td><td>Varies by tune</td><td></td><td>How a song leaves home and comes back.</td><td>“Blue Moon”, “Satin Doll”, “Take the A Train”.</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Vamp and release</td><td>Varies by tune</td><td>Home or a bond for the vamp, a molecule for the release</td><td>Varies by tune</td><td></td><td>Harmonic stillness against harmonic motion within one piece.</td><td>“Superstition” (vamp against chorus), “Chameleon”, much funk and Afrobeat arrangement.</td></tr><tr><td>Compound</td><td>Moving home (Key changes)</td><td>From C: A♭7 → D♭ to establish D♭ major, or A7 → D to establish D major. A direct C → D♭ or C → D shift is also possible; G7 normally leads to C.</td><td>Familiar chord relationships reinterpreted around a new tonic; often prepared by its dominant</td><td>Varies by tune</td><td></td><td>How home itself moves.</td><td>“Love on Top” (repeated lifts), “Man in the Mirror”.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"ref-the-lineages","title":"The lineages","html":"<p>How elements descend from one another. Each line starts with something small and changes one thing at a time. These are the relationships to point out whenever a new element arrives: where it came from, and what changed.</p><div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Lineage</th><th scope=\"col\">Path</th><th scope=\"col\">What it shows</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>The parent line</td><td>I IV and I V → I IV V → I7 IV7 V7 → Twelve bar blues</td><td>Lean and pull supply the major collection. Blues sevenths add chromatic colour while home can remain clear.</td></tr><tr><td>The blues line</td><td>I7 → I7 IV7 → Twelve bar blues → Twelve bar blues, revisited → Jazz blues</td><td>A lean bond grows into a form, then absorbs substitutions and secondary dominants.</td></tr><tr><td>The cadence line</td><td>I V → ii V held open → ii V I → ii V I IV → iii vi ii V I → III7 VI7 II7 V7 I → Rhythm changes bridge</td><td>The pull bond, displaced, then chained, then made chromatic.</td></tr><tr><td>The chair swap</td><td>i7 IV7 ⇄ ii V held open → ii V I</td><td>The same two chords with a different home, and then the arrival that settles it.</td></tr><tr><td>The minor line</td><td>i v → i V → i iv v and i iv V → iiø7 V7 i → Two homes with one altered note → Minor blues</td><td>One note, the raised seventh, runs through the whole line.</td></tr><tr><td>The shadow line</td><td>I vi → I vi IV V → I vi ii V → I VI7 ii V → Rhythm changes A section</td><td>A shadow bond, then substitution, then a secondary dominant.</td></tr><tr><td>The axis line</td><td>I vi IV V → I V vi IV → vi IV I V</td><td>Reordering and rotating the chords changes emphasis; it may change the perceived tonic.</td></tr><tr><td>The borrowed line</td><td>I IV → I iv → IV iv I → iv7 ♭VII7 I</td><td>One note borrowed from the parallel minor, then two.</td></tr><tr><td>The Mixolydian line</td><td>I7 → I v → I ♭VII → I ♭VII IV</td><td>The ♭7 as a colour of home rather than a tension.</td></tr><tr><td>The modal minor line</td><td>i → i ♭VII → i ♭VII ♭VI ♭VII → i ♭VII ♭VI V</td><td>Stepwise motion throughout, until a real dominant appears.</td></tr><tr><td>The mediant line</td><td>I vi and I iii → I ♭VI, I ♭III, I III → I III IV iv</td><td>Diatonic shadows, then their chromatic cousins.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"ref-a-reading-by-genre","title":"A reading by genre","html":"<p>The course is ordered by relationship, but part of the familiar historical story survives on its own, and it is worth telling once.</p><p>Blues arrives as the first compound in Stage 4 because a basic form uses home, lean and pull. Its use of a dominant-seventh tonic and the interaction of major and minor inflections deserve explicit attention. Other styles also have distinctive harmonic practices; this course uses a limited set of comparisons rather than a complete history of harmony.</p><p>The doo wop, Motown and standards material clusters in Stage 6, because it depends on the relative minor and on substitution. Soul's chromatic colour arrives in Stages 7 to 9: the borrowed iv and the backdoor cadence as colours, the secondary dominants as applied chords. Modal rock and folk wait for the colour bonds in Stages 7 and 8.</p><p>Funk and Afrobeat break the chronology completely. Their core materials (the static minor home and the Dorian lean) sit in Stages 1 and 2. James Brown's early hits were twelve bar blues in funk clothes, and the leap from blues to funk was rhythmic long before it was harmonic. Nobody has to wait until they reach funk chronologically in order to play it.</p><p>The pop axis uses the same four chords as the fifties progression in a different order. Compare the bass motion and phrase endings: shared ingredients do not guarantee the same function or effect.</p>"},{"id":"ref-backing-track-plan","title":"Backing track plan","html":"<p>Preparation plan for Logic; audio files are still to be produced. Build controlled loops first in C and transpose the MIDI parts, checking register and voicings in each key. Keep tempo, groove, level and instrumentation matched within each comparison pair. Start with a small pack for the core route, then expand as teaching requires.</p><p><b>Track types.</b> A loop repeats one element. A layering track alternates a parent bond with a larger progression. A contrast track alternates matched versions. A quiz track randomises the taught contrasts and has a separate answer key. An ambiguity track compares different emphases or endings without pretending that every loop has one provable tonic.</p><p><b>Production requirements.</b> Give each file an element ID, key, exact voicings, tempo, metre and bars per loop. Include a count-in, a complete form and a clear ending. Provide a version without bass for playing and a reference version with bass for checking; export quiz answers separately. Avoid busy melodies on open-degree tests, and ensure the accompaniment actually changes for substitution exercises. Recordings and listening timestamps must be checked before a student edition is issued.</p><div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Stage</th><th scope=\"col\">Tracks needed</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>1. Home</td><td>One long drone track per home quality (I, I7, i), lightly pulsed, no form. Around five minutes each.</td></tr><tr><td>2. Lean</td><td>One loop per bond (two bars per chord) in triads and in sevenths. An ambiguous version of I IV and i7 IV7 with the bass and groove weighted towards the second chord. A Lean quiz track with the four bonds in random eight bar blocks.</td></tr><tr><td>3. Pull</td><td>One loop per bond in triads and sevenths. A pair track for i7 IV7 and ii V held open on the same chords, with bass and groove placing home differently. A Pull quiz track, and a combined Lean and Pull quiz track.</td></tr><tr><td>4. First molecules</td><td>One loop per molecule. A layering track for each (the parent bond for eight bars, then the molecule for eight). A contrast track alternating i iv v and i iv V. The twelve bar blues in shuffle and straight feels.</td></tr><tr><td>5. Shadow</td><td>One loop per bond in triads and sevenths. A Shadow quiz track, and a combined quiz across all bonds from Stages 2, 3 and 5.</td></tr><tr><td>6. Shadow molecules</td><td>One loop per molecule. Substitution pair tracks (I vi IV V against I vi ii V, and I IV V against I iii IV V). A rotation track for the pop axis, with the same four chords and the bass entering on a different chord each section.</td></tr><tr><td>7. Colour</td><td>One loop per bond in triads and sevenths. A Colour quiz track, and the full bond quiz across all the relationships.</td></tr><tr><td>8. Colour molecules</td><td>One loop per molecule. A contrast track alternating i ♭VII ♭VI ♭VII with the Andalusian cadence.</td></tr><tr><td>9. Applied</td><td>A before and after pair track for each applied chord and each transformation, each also in a randomised order version.</td></tr><tr><td>10. Compounds</td><td>Each full form in at least two feels. A version of the blues forms with random substitutions from Stage 9, for call the changes practice.</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"ref-appendix-a-diatonic-sevenths-twelve-majo","title":"Appendix A. Diatonic sevenths, twelve major keys","html":"<p>Every major key element can be spelled from this table.</p><div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Key</th><th scope=\"col\">Imaj7</th><th scope=\"col\">iim7</th><th scope=\"col\">iiim7</th><th scope=\"col\">IVmaj7</th><th scope=\"col\">V7</th><th scope=\"col\">vim7</th><th scope=\"col\">viiø7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>C</td><td>Cmaj7</td><td>Dm7</td><td>Em7</td><td>Fmaj7</td><td>G7</td><td>Am7</td><td>Bø7</td></tr><tr><td>F</td><td>Fmaj7</td><td>Gm7</td><td>Am7</td><td>B♭maj7</td><td>C7</td><td>Dm7</td><td>Eø7</td></tr><tr><td>B♭</td><td>B♭maj7</td><td>Cm7</td><td>Dm7</td><td>E♭maj7</td><td>F7</td><td>Gm7</td><td>Aø7</td></tr><tr><td>E♭</td><td>E♭maj7</td><td>Fm7</td><td>Gm7</td><td>A♭maj7</td><td>B♭7</td><td>Cm7</td><td>Dø7</td></tr><tr><td>A♭</td><td>A♭maj7</td><td>B♭m7</td><td>Cm7</td><td>D♭maj7</td><td>E♭7</td><td>Fm7</td><td>Gø7</td></tr><tr><td>D♭</td><td>D♭maj7</td><td>E♭m7</td><td>Fm7</td><td>G♭maj7</td><td>A♭7</td><td>B♭m7</td><td>Cø7</td></tr><tr><td>G♭</td><td>G♭maj7</td><td>A♭m7</td><td>B♭m7</td><td>C♭maj7</td><td>D♭7</td><td>E♭m7</td><td>Fø7</td></tr><tr><td>B</td><td>Bmaj7</td><td>C♯m7</td><td>D♯m7</td><td>Emaj7</td><td>F♯7</td><td>G♯m7</td><td>A♯ø7</td></tr><tr><td>E</td><td>Emaj7</td><td>F♯m7</td><td>G♯m7</td><td>Amaj7</td><td>B7</td><td>C♯m7</td><td>D♯ø7</td></tr><tr><td>A</td><td>Amaj7</td><td>Bm7</td><td>C♯m7</td><td>Dmaj7</td><td>E7</td><td>F♯m7</td><td>G♯ø7</td></tr><tr><td>D</td><td>Dmaj7</td><td>Em7</td><td>F♯m7</td><td>Gmaj7</td><td>A7</td><td>Bm7</td><td>C♯ø7</td></tr><tr><td>G</td><td>Gmaj7</td><td>Am7</td><td>Bm7</td><td>Cmaj7</td><td>D7</td><td>Em7</td><td>F♯ø7</td></tr></tbody></table></div><p>G♭ is often easier read as F♯: F♯maj7, G♯m7, A♯m7, Bmaj7, C♯7, D♯m7, E♯ø7.</p>"},{"id":"ref-appendix-b-the-practical-minor-set-twelv","title":"Appendix B. The practical minor set, twelve keys","html":"<p>This practical minor-key set combines natural-minor chords with a major V7 formed by raising degree 7. In C minor, G–B–D–F uses B natural. This is a normal minor-key alteration, not by itself a modulation. For i–v or i–iv–v, use the unaltered minor v7 instead. The sharp minor keys are enharmonic spellings of positions in the cycle, chosen to keep chord names readable.</p><div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Key</th><th scope=\"col\">i7</th><th scope=\"col\">iiø7</th><th scope=\"col\">♭IIImaj7</th><th scope=\"col\">iv7</th><th scope=\"col\">V7</th><th scope=\"col\">♭VImaj7</th><th scope=\"col\">♭VII7</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Cm</td><td>Cm7</td><td>Dø7</td><td>E♭maj7</td><td>Fm7</td><td>G7</td><td>A♭maj7</td><td>B♭7</td></tr><tr><td>Fm</td><td>Fm7</td><td>Gø7</td><td>A♭maj7</td><td>B♭m7</td><td>C7</td><td>D♭maj7</td><td>E♭7</td></tr><tr><td>B♭m</td><td>B♭m7</td><td>Cø7</td><td>D♭maj7</td><td>E♭m7</td><td>F7</td><td>G♭maj7</td><td>A♭7</td></tr><tr><td>E♭m</td><td>E♭m7</td><td>Fø7</td><td>G♭maj7</td><td>A♭m7</td><td>B♭7</td><td>C♭maj7</td><td>D♭7</td></tr><tr><td>G♯m</td><td>G♯m7</td><td>A♯ø7</td><td>Bmaj7</td><td>C♯m7</td><td>D♯7</td><td>Emaj7</td><td>F♯7</td></tr><tr><td>C♯m</td><td>C♯m7</td><td>D♯ø7</td><td>Emaj7</td><td>F♯m7</td><td>G♯7</td><td>Amaj7</td><td>B7</td></tr><tr><td>F♯m</td><td>F♯m7</td><td>G♯ø7</td><td>Amaj7</td><td>Bm7</td><td>C♯7</td><td>Dmaj7</td><td>E7</td></tr><tr><td>Bm</td><td>Bm7</td><td>C♯ø7</td><td>Dmaj7</td><td>Em7</td><td>F♯7</td><td>Gmaj7</td><td>A7</td></tr><tr><td>Em</td><td>Em7</td><td>F♯ø7</td><td>Gmaj7</td><td>Am7</td><td>B7</td><td>Cmaj7</td><td>D7</td></tr><tr><td>Am</td><td>Am7</td><td>Bø7</td><td>Cmaj7</td><td>Dm7</td><td>E7</td><td>Fmaj7</td><td>G7</td></tr><tr><td>Dm</td><td>Dm7</td><td>Eø7</td><td>Fmaj7</td><td>Gm7</td><td>A7</td><td>B♭maj7</td><td>C7</td></tr><tr><td>Gm</td><td>Gm7</td><td>Aø7</td><td>B♭maj7</td><td>Cm7</td><td>D7</td><td>E♭maj7</td><td>F7</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"ref-appendix-c-chord-scale-reference","title":"Appendix C. Chord scale reference","html":"<div class=\"he-table\"><table><thead><tr><th scope=\"col\">Chord</th><th scope=\"col\">Typical role</th><th scope=\"col\">Scale</th><th scope=\"col\">Formula</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>maj7</td><td>I (tonic)</td><td>Ionian</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 7</td></tr><tr><td>maj7</td><td>IV, or I with a ♯4 colour</td><td>Lydian</td><td>1 2 3 ♯4 5 6 7</td></tr><tr><td>m7</td><td>ii, or i in the Dorian lean</td><td>Dorian</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 6 ♭7</td></tr><tr><td>m7</td><td>iii</td><td>Phrygian</td><td>1 ♭2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7</td></tr><tr><td>m7</td><td>vi, or i in modal minor</td><td>Aeolian</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7</td></tr><tr><td>m6, mMaj7</td><td>i in functional minor</td><td>Melodic minor</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 6 7</td></tr><tr><td>m, mMaj7</td><td>i with a V7 nearby</td><td>Harmonic minor</td><td>1 2 ♭3 4 5 ♭6 7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>V in a major key, I7 as home</td><td>Mixolydian</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 ♭7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>V in a minor key, secondary dominants into minor chords</td><td>Phrygian dominant</td><td>1 ♭2 3 4 5 ♭6 ♭7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>Possible colour on II7, ♭VII7 or ♭II7; check function and melody</td><td>Lydian dominant</td><td>1 2 3 ♯4 5 6 ♭7</td></tr><tr><td>7</td><td>I7 or IV7 in blues</td><td>Mixolydian with the minor pentatonic over it</td><td>1 2 3 4 5 6 ♭7, plus ♭3</td></tr><tr><td>ø7</td><td>vii in a major key</td><td>Locrian</td><td>1 ♭2 ♭3 4 ♭5 ♭6 ♭7</td></tr><tr><td>ø7</td><td>ii in harmonic-minor context; Locrian is another common starting point</td><td>Locrian ♮6</td><td>1 ♭2 ♭3 4 ♭5 6 ♭7</td></tr><tr><td>7alt</td><td>V with altered ninth/fifth colours</td><td>Altered</td><td>1 ♭9 ♯9 3 ♭5 ♯5 ♭7</td></tr></tbody></table></div>"},{"id":"ref-appendix-d-worked-example-a-bond-i7-iv7-","title":"Appendix D. Worked example: a bond, i7 IV7 in C","html":"<p><b>The chords.</b> Cm7 (C E♭ G B♭), F7 (F A C E♭).</p><p><b>The three questions.</b> Expanded voicing the bond fixes 1, ♭3, 4, 5, 6 and ♭7. Only the 2 is open, and it is almost always natural. The fixed ♮6 (the A in F7) is what makes the bond Dorian.</p><p><b>Shared and moving tones.</b> C and E♭ are common to Cm7 and F7. One compact mapping holds C and E♭, moves G down to F and B♭ down to A. The last move connects Cm7’s seventh to F7’s third.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Horizontal ladder</h3><ul><li>Roots: C F</li><li>Thirds: E♭ A</li><li>Fifths: G C</li><li>Sevenths: B♭ E♭</li><li>Guide tones, third then seventh: E♭ held as E♭ (third of Cm7 becomes seventh of F7)</li><li>Guide tones, seventh then third: B♭ falls to A</li></ul><p><b>Chord scales.</b> C Dorian (C D E♭ F G A B♭) over Cm7 and F Mixolydian (F G A B♭ C D E♭) over F7 are the same seven notes. Note choice is about targeting, not changing scale.</p><p><b>Whose home.</b> The same two chords are ii V in B♭. The groove and bass decide whether this is a Dorian vamp or a cadence held open.</p><p><b>Ear test.</b> Compare A and A♭ over Cm7 first. Then change the accompaniment from F7 to Fm7: its third moves A→A♭. Playing A♭ against an unchanged F7 creates a clash or deliberate colour; it does not turn the band’s chord into Fm7.</p>"},{"id":"ref-appendix-e-worked-example-ii-v-i-in-c","title":"Appendix E. Worked example: ii V I in C","html":"<p><b>The chords.</b> Dm7 (D F A C), G7 (G B D F), Cmaj7 (C E G B).</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Vertical ladder</h3><ul><li>Roots: D | G | C</li><li>1 5 8: D A D | G D G | C G C</li><li>1 3 5: D F A | G B D | C E G</li><li>1 3 5 7: D F A C | G B D F | C E G B</li><li>3 5 7 1: F A C D | B D F G | E G B C</li><li>5 7 1 3: A C D F | D F G B | G B C E</li><li>7 1 3 5: C D F A | F G B D | B C E G</li><li>7 5 3 1 (descending): C A F D | F D B G | B G E C</li><li>5 3 7 1 (scramble): A F C D | D B F G | G E B C</li><li>Tenths, 1 10: D up to F | G up to B | C up to E</li><li>Tenths, 1 5 10: D A F | G D B | C G E</li></ul><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Horizontal ladder</h3><ul><li>Roots: D G C</li><li>Thirds: F B E</li><li>Fifths: A D G</li><li>Sevenths: C F B</li><li>Ninths: E A D</li></ul><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Guide tone lines</h3><ul><li>Third, seventh, third: F, F, E. A common tone, then a semitone down.</li><li>Seventh, third, seventh: C, B, B. A semitone down, then a common tone.</li></ul><p>The two lines contain two semitone moves in total, with the other two transitions held as common tones. Play each line separately, then alternate between them. Appendix H shows notation and TAB.</p><p><b>Chord scales.</b> D Dorian, G Mixolydian and C Ionian contain the same seven pitches. Their names describe each collection relative to the current chord root. The tonal centre can remain C throughout; changing chord-relative labels is not a change of key.</p>"},{"id":"ref-appendix-f-worked-example-ii-7-v7-i-in-c","title":"Appendix F. Worked example: iiø7 V7 i in C minor","html":"<p><b>The chords.</b> Dø7 (D–F–A♭–C), G7 (G–B–D–F), Cm7 (C–E♭–G–B♭). Alternative tonic voicings include Cm6 (C–E♭–G–A) and CmMaj7 (C–E♭–G–B); choose the tonic collection to match the actual voicing.</p><ul><li>Roots: D G C</li><li>Thirds: F B E♭</li><li>Fifths: A♭ D G</li><li>Sevenths: C F B♭</li><li>Guide tones, third, seventh, third: F, F, E♭</li><li>Guide tones, seventh, third, seventh: C, B, B♭</li></ul><p><b>Chord scales.</b> D Locrian ♮6 (D–E♭–F–G–A♭–B–C) and G Phrygian dominant (G–A♭–B–C–D–E♭–F) are modes of C harmonic minor. Over Cm7, C Aeolian supplies B♭. Over CmMaj7, harmonic minor supplies B; over Cm6, a collection containing A, such as C melodic minor, may be appropriate. Test the sixth and seventh against the accompaniment.</p><p>Harmonic minor covers the chord tones of the approach and dominant. It does not fit the seventh of Cm7 unchanged: B becomes B♭ on that tonic. Minor keys routinely use variable sixths and sevenths. C remains home unless the phrase establishes another centre.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">A practical example in E♭ minor</h3><p>In the course’s “It’s a Man’s Man’s Man’s World” arrangement, the vamp is E♭m–B♭m (i–v) and the turnaround A♭m–B♭7 (iv–V7), returning to E♭m. E♭ natural minor is E♭–F–G♭–A♭–B♭–C♭–D♭. On B♭7, raise D♭ to D natural; the other notes stay. This gives E♭ harmonic minor, or B♭ Phrygian dominant when numbered from the dominant root.</p><p>The relative major is G♭ major, not F♯ major in this spelling. That shared collection helps you map the minor-key chords, but it does not account for B♭7’s D natural without an alteration. The raised seventh strengthens the return to E♭; it is not a key change. Over B♭7, C♭ is ♭9 and G♭ is ♭13. Resolve C♭ down to B♭, and hear G♭ become the tonic chord’s minor third. Appendix H isolates the third change in notation and TAB.</p>"},{"id":"ref-appendix-g-the-twelve-key-order","title":"Appendix G. The twelve key order","html":"<p>C, F, B♭, E♭, A♭, D♭, G♭ (or F♯), B, E, A, D, G, and back to C.</p><p>The next key in this practice order is a fourth higher, equivalent in pitch class to a fifth lower. That rehearses a common cadential root relationship. Use a short V7–I or V7–i lead-in to hear each new tonic; merely transposing an exercise does not make the sequence itself a cadence.</p>"},{"id":"ref-appendix-h-bass-notation-studies","title":"Appendix H. Bass notation studies","html":"<p>These are original practice studies, not transcriptions of the listening leads. All notes are crotchets in 4/4, with one chord per bar. Standard bass notation is written one octave above sounding pitch; TAB gives the literal frets on E–A–D–G strings. Start around 60 bpm or slower if needed. Sing or match one line, play the study over the named chords, then invent a two-bar phrase using its target.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Study 1  The Dorian lean</h3><p>Use with Stage 2 and Appendix D. In Cm7–F7, E♭ stays while B♭ falls to A. Play the notes on beats 1 and 3 as one line, then those on 2 and 4 as the other. The line beginning on E♭ changes role from the third of Cm7 to the seventh of F7.</p><p><b>Listen and apply.</b> Keep the accompaniment on Cm7–F7 and sing A before F7 arrives. Then compare Cm7–Fm7 with matching accompaniment. Hear A→A♭ in the chord’s third.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Study 2  The major cadence</h3><p>Use with Stage 4 and Appendix E. The lower line is F–F–E and the upper line C–B–B. Each has one semitone move and one common tone: two semitone moves in total. The exercise alternates between the voices; it is not a single chromatic line.</p><p><b>Listen and apply.</b> Sing one voice while playing the other. Then make a bassline that starts each bar on its root and reaches one of these guide tones before the next change.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Study 3  The minor cadence</h3><p>Use with Stage 4 and Appendix F. Compare directly with Study 2: Dm7 becomes Dm7♭5 in the accompaniment, G7 stays, and Cmaj7 becomes Cm7. The guide-tone lines become F–F–E♭ and C–B–B♭. The ♭5 of Dm7♭5 is supplied by the backing, not by this guide-tone line.</p><p><b>Listen and apply.</b> Count one held transition, two semitone moves and one whole-tone move across the two voices. Then change the final chord to CmMaj7: which last note in the upper voice must change? Answer: B♭ becomes B.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Study 4  The minor dominant</h3><p>Use with Stage 3 and the E♭ minor example in Appendix F. This isolated comparison places B♭m beside B♭7 before E♭m; it is not the song’s complete chord order. First compare just their thirds, D♭ and D. The arpeggio then adds A♭ to spell B♭7 explicitly.</p><p><b>Listen and apply.</b> Keep E♭ as home. Over B♭7, sing D→E♭, then C♭→B♭. Return to the actual vamp E♭m–B♭m and turnaround A♭m–B♭7. G♭ stays in the parent collection and becomes the third of E♭m; no new tonic is required.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Study 5  The borrowed minor iv</h3><p>Use with Stage 9. The upper voice A–A♭–G traces F–Fm–C while the lower C is held. This is a voice-leading reduction: hear it over the complete triads before using the line on its own.</p><p><b>Listen and apply.</b> Compare F–C with F–Fm–C at the same tempo. Write a phrase that lets A♭ resolve to G, then place that movement inside a bassline with roots and rests.</p><h3 class=\"he-g-h\">Teaching checks and further reading</h3><p>Before assigning a study, confirm the accompaniment’s chord symbols and voicings. Count the written bars and demonstrate a complete loop. Let the student choose a comfortable fingering; the TAB is one workable route, not a compulsory position.</p><p>Every vertical bar in the chord charts marks one complete bar. Unless a chart says otherwise, use 4/4; two chord symbols within one bar receive two beats each. The compound charts are teaching arrangements. Chord durations in recordings may differ.</p><p><a href=\"https://viva.pressbooks.pub/openmusictheory/chapter/minor-scales/\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Music Theory — Minor Scales, Scale Degrees, and Key Signatures.</a> Reference for natural, harmonic and melodic minor.</p><p><a href=\"https://openmusictheory.github.io/appliedChords.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Music Theory — Applied chords.</a> Reference for applied dominants and the distinction between local tonicisation and modulation.</p><p><a href=\"https://openmusictheory.github.io/tendencyTonesFunctionalDissonances.html\" target=\"_blank\" rel=\"noopener\">Open Music Theory — Tendency tones and functional harmonic dissonances.</a> Reference for the context-dependent behaviour of tendency tones.</p><p>Melodic minor in this course uses the jazz convention of the ascending collection in both directions. In classical scalar exercises, melodic minor is often taught with natural minor on descent; actual melodic practice is more flexible than that exercise convention.</p>"}],"title":"Harmonic Elements","methodTitle":"Part One. The Method","elementsTitle":"Part Two. The Elements","elementsIntro":"<p>Ten stages. Each opens with what it is about, then gives every element a card. The card's facts are all in C. The prose underneath follows the unit steps: what it sounds like, how note choice works over it, what to listen for, what to contrast it with, and where to hear it.</p>","referenceTitle":"Part Three. Reference","uncarded":["sus-2-home","sus-4-home","sixth-home","augmented-home","diminished-home","twelve-bar-blues","blues-revisited","minor-blues","jazz-blues","aeolian-cadence","dorian-loop","minor-rock-cell","dorian-shift","fifties-in-minor","rock-cell","mixolydian-sway","axis-lean-first","falling-shadows","axis-with-ii","climb","walk-down","deceptive-lift","gospel-turn","three-six-two-five","ii-v-into-iv","diminished-passing-chord","minor-blues-turn","line-cliche"]};
}(typeof window !== 'undefined' ? window : globalThis));
